{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat AGENTS.md'","aggregated_output":"# Working in this repository\n\nAxylo is a browser charting library. The product is specified in `docs/02-product-spec.md`; the build plan is `docs/03-plan-typescript-release.md`. Read the sections your task names before writing code.\n\n## Rules for every change\n\n- **Contracts are fixed.** `packages/axylo/src/core/contracts.ts` and `packages/axylo/src/public.ts` are the interfaces between modules. Do not edit them. If your work needs a change there, stop that part, finish what you can, and describe the exact change you need in your report.\n- **Stay in your files.** Each task lists the directories it owns. Do not create, edit or delete anything else, including configuration and other modules. Several agents work in this checkout at the same time.\n- **No runtime dependencies.** Use the platform (`Intl`, typed arrays). Dev dependencies already installed: TypeScript, Vitest, fast-check, esbuild, Playwright (system Chrome), axe-core. The network is not available; do not try to install anything.\n- **No DOM at import time.** A module that needs the DOM takes it as a parameter or touches it inside a function. Pure stages (data, scales, formatting, layout, scene) must run in Node.\n- **Hot paths use typed arrays.** No object per data point, no array of arrays per data point, no copying of large columns that the contracts say are borrowed.\n- **Never guess silently.** Anything that cannot be drawn as given is reported through the `DiagnosticSink` with the code from spec section 3.5.\n- **Tests come from the spec.** Expected values are derived from the specification and the fixture data, never by running the implementation and pasting its output. Use fast-check property tests for invariants (\"for any data …\"). A test you cannot make pass is reported as failing; do not weaken it or change its expected value.\n- **TypeScript strict, no `any`** in exported signatures. `__DEV__` is a build-time boolean (true in tests).\n- **Comments say why**, not what. Names are plain words.\n- **Do not commit.** The coordinator commits.\n\n## Commands\n\n```bash\nnpm run typecheck      # tsc over the library, its tests, and the API examples in docs/api\nnpm test               # unit and property tests (Node)\nnpm run build          # packages/axylo/dist\nnpm run test:browser   # browser tests against the built bundle, in system Chrome\n```\n\n## Your report\n\nEnd with a short report: what you built (files), what you verified and how (paste the last lines of the test run), what is not done or not verified, any contract change you need, and any place where the spec was ambiguous and what you assumed. Keep \"ran and saw\" apart from \"expect\".\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat reviews/2026-11-19/wp/rehearsal-harness.prompt.md reviews/2026-11-19/wp/rehearsal-harness.report.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat reviews/2026-11-19/wp/rehearsal-harness.prompt.md reviews/2026-11-19/wp/rehearsal-harness.report.md'","aggregated_output":"# Build the agent rehearsal harness (spec DX12)\n\nYou are working in /home/canarit/projects/axylo (Axylo, a TypeScript browser charting library). Read `AGENTS.md`, then the DX12 row of `docs/02-product-spec.md`, then the documentation pages an author starts from: `apps/docs/pages/getting-started.md`, `data.md`, `diagnostics.md`, `line.md`, `bar.md`. You own a new top-level directory `rehearsal/`; create nothing outside it, except one line in the root `.gitignore` if you need to ignore run workspaces. Do not edit `packages/**`, `apps/**`, `acceptance/**`, `tests/**`. You cannot commit (`.git` is read-only for you); the coordinator commits. The network is off: do not install anything (dev dependencies present: TypeScript, esbuild, playwright-core with system Chrome via `channel: 'chrome'`, Vitest). Do not run `npm run build` (it rewrites the shared `packages/axylo/dist`).\n\n## What DX12 asks\n\n\"A coding agent gets the first chart right. Given fixed prompts and trap fixtures (UTC-midnight ISO dates, numeric strings, unsorted rows, missing days, a misspelled field), an agent with only the published docs produces a chart with no error diagnostics that passes the reader criteria. Recorded as agent rehearsal, not as user evidence.\"\n\nThe harness runs a coding agent on a task, then grades what it wrote, automatically and the same way every time. The coordinator runs it; you build and test it. The agents under test are command-line coding agents (Codex CLI and Claude Code); you do not need to run them (and should not: they need the network), but the harness must invoke them.\n\n## Design (implement this; if a part cannot work as written, say why and do the closest honest thing)\n\n1. **Fixtures**, `rehearsal/fixtures/`: small realistic JSON files, one trap or more each, values chosen so that a mistake is visible in the result:\n   - `daily-visits.json`: 30 days of `{ \"day\": \"2026-03-01T00:00:00Z\", \"visits\": 1234 }` (instants at UTC midnight that mean calendar days; read in America/Los_Angeles they fall on the previous day: the trap), with three days missing (absent rows, not nulls) and the rows shuffled.\n   - `regional-sales.json`: quarterly sales by region where values are numeric strings (`\"1250.5\"`), one region per row field.\n   - `signups.json`: daily signups where the field is spelled `signpus` in the data while the prompt says \"signups\" (the misspelled field: the agent must notice the diagnostic, AX101, and use the real key, not invent data).\n   - one combined fixture with several traps at once.\n   Write each fixture's expected reading in `rehearsal/fixtures/<name>.expected.json`: what the chart's data table (spec A4, the \"View as table\" table) must show, row by row, as calendar dates or categories and numbers, derived by hand from the fixture's meaning (missing days are absent or \"No data\", never 0; dates are the calendar days the data means; numbers are numbers). Do not derive expected values by running Axylo.\n2. **Prompts**, `rehearsal/prompts/<id>.md`, one per task (4 to 6 tasks), written as a developer would ask, in plain words, naming the data file and the chart wanted (\"Show daily visits from data.json as a line chart titled 'Daily visits'.\"), never mentioning the trap. Each prompt also states the delivery contract (below) and that the only documentation available is in `docs/`.\n3. **Workspace per run**, created by the runner outside the repository (in a directory the caller gives, default the session's scratch directory passed with `--workdir`): `docs/` = a copy of the built documentation site (`apps/docs/dist`, already built; copy, do not build), `node_modules/axylo` = the packed tarball unpacked (`tests/dist/.packed/axylo-0.0.0.tgz`, already packed), `data.json` = the fixture, `PROMPT.md` = the prompt, and a minimal `package.json` with `\"type\": \"module\"`. Nothing from the repository's source. The delivery contract: the agent writes `chart.js`, an ES module whose default export is `function render(element, data)` that draws the chart into `element` and returns the chart handle; it may use `import { line, bar, … } from 'axylo'`. It may run `node` (for example `validate` from `axylo`, which the docs describe) to check its work.\n4. **Runner**, `rehearsal/run.mjs`: `node rehearsal/run.mjs --agent codex|claude --model <id> [--effort <e>] --task <id> --workdir <dir>`; prepares the workspace, runs the agent non-interactively in it with the prompt, with a time limit (default 20 minutes), and keeps the agent's full transcript:\n   - codex: `codex exec -m <model> [-c model_reasoning_effort=\"<e>\"] -s workspace-write --skip-git-repo-check -C <workspace> --json - < PROMPT.md > transcript.jsonl` (the observed model and effort are in `~/.codex/sessions/**/rollout-*-<thread_id>.jsonl`, `turn_context`; record them; the thread id is the first `--json` event);\n   - claude: `claude -p --model <model> --output-format stream-json --verbose --allowedTools \"Read,Write,Edit,Glob,Grep,Bash(node:*),Bash(ls:*),Bash(cat:*)\" < PROMPT.md > transcript.jsonl` with the workspace as the working directory (observed model: the `model` field of the assistant messages).\n   - **Audit**: after the run, scan the transcript for any file path outside the workspace that the agent read or ran (command lines, tool inputs): a run that touched the Axylo repository or anything outside the workspace other than system tools is recorded as void, with the paths.\n5. **Grader**, `rehearsal/grade.mjs` (the runner calls it; it can also be run alone on a workspace): bundles `chart.js` with esbuild for the browser (as a consumer would, `conditions: ['browser']`, the production condition), loads it in system Chrome (playwright-core, headless, `channel: 'chrome'`) into a page with `<div id=\"chart\" style=\"width:720px;height:400px\">`, calls `render`, and judges, in two time zones (`America/Los_Angeles` and `Asia/Tokyo`, via the context's `timezoneId`):\n   - **no error diagnostics**: no console message carrying an Axylo error-level code (the registry `packages/axylo/src/diagnostics/registry.ts` gives levels; production prints errors and warnings once per code) and the chart is not in its error state; warnings and hints are listed in the record, not failed, except AX140 (no title) which fails the reader criterion below;\n   - **reader criteria**: the figure has an accessible name that is the title the prompt asked for (not the generated fallback, AX140); opening \"View as table\" shows exactly the expected rows of `<fixture>.expected.json` in both time zones (dates as the calendar days, missing days not shown as 0, values as numbers formatted as the table formats them: compare numbers by value, not by string); no \"Values not shown\" note unless the expected file says one is right; the chart type is the one asked for.\n   - If `chart.js` is missing, does not export `render`, or throws: fail, with the reason.\n   - Output: `grade.json` in the workspace: `{ pass, criteria: [{ name, pass, detail }], diagnostics: [...], timezones: [...] }`.\n6. **Record**: the runner copies the transcript, the agent's `chart.js`, `grade.json` and a `run.json` (agent, requested and observed model and effort, task, start and end times, duration, exit, void or not with paths) into `rehearsal/records/<date>-<agent>-<task>-<n>/` inside the repository, so the record survives a restart. `node rehearsal/summary.mjs` prints a table of all records (agent, model, task, pass, failed criteria).\n\n## Tests\n\nVitest tests in `rehearsal/test/` (run with `npx vitest run --root rehearsal` or a config there; do not change the root config): the grader on hand-written `chart.js` answers: a correct one per fixture passes; and mutations each fail the right criterion: UTC-midnight read as instants (Los Angeles shows the previous day), numeric strings not parsed, missing days filled with 0, the misspelled field used as given (AX101), no title, wrong chart type. The fixtures' expected files are checked against the fixtures by an independent small computation in the test (not by Axylo). The runner's audit on two synthetic transcripts (one clean, one that reads `/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/index.ts`). If the browser cannot be launched from your sandbox, write the browser tests anyway, mark which you could not run, and say so; the coordinator runs them.\n\n## Report (your final message)\n\nFiles; how to run one rehearsal; what you ran and saw (last lines); what you could not run; any place where the design above was not workable and what you did instead.\n<!-- Author: Codex, requested gpt-6.2-sol xhigh; observed gpt-6.2-sol, effort xhigh (session rollout turn_context). workspace-write. Verified by the coordinator: npx vitest run --root rehearsal, 31 of 31 pass including the 15 browser grading cases the sandbox could not run. Design by the coordinator (Claude Opus 5.5); design review by gpt-6.2-astra follows. Saved by the coordinator. -->\n\nImplemented DX12 in [rehearsal/](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/). It includes four fixed prompts and fixtures with hand-written expected rows, a [runner](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/run.mjs) for Codex and Claude, a [grader](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/grade.mjs) for Los Angeles and Tokyo, and a [summary](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/summary.mjs) of saved records.\n\n```sh\nnode rehearsal/run.mjs --agent codex --model YOUR_MODEL_ID --effort high \\\n  --task daily-visits --workdir /tmp/axylo-runs\nnode rehearsal/summary.mjs\n```\n\nA local stub verified invocation, transcript capture, model recording, and the summary. The non-browser tests passed.\n\n```text\nTest Files  2 passed (2)\n     Tests  16 passed (16)\n```\n\nChrome failed to launch in the sandbox with `setsockopt: Operation not permitted`, so the 15 browser cases remain unverified. No live agent runs were made.\n\nThe visits fixture covers 30 calendar days with 27 observations. The grader accepts each missing day as absent or “No data.” Axylo automatically parses fields made entirely of decimal strings, so the unparsed-string failure test uses mixed numbers and strings in the combined fixture. The workspace copy preserves published examples and omits internal source snapshots. The audit checks paths visible in transcripts. The harness uses the existing public contracts.","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
