{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n -i 'reader criteria|readers?|rehearsal|DX12' docs/02-product-spec.md | head -110\"","aggregated_output":"5:Based on the findings in [01-landscape.md](01-landscape.md). Finding IDs (R1–R16 for readers, D1–D10 for developers) refer to that document. Criterion IDs defined here are F, L, I, A (reader), DX (developer) and P (performance).\n9:**Axylo is the everyday chart you can ship without checking it.** The smallest correct call produces a chart that represents the data faithfully, stays readable at any width and in any color scheme, and works with a keyboard and a screen reader. When the data is wrong, Axylo says so, to the developer and to the reader, instead of drawing a guess.\n21:| Chart.js | Simple API, permissive license, widest reach | Keyboard and screen-reader access without author work; reduced-motion support; a tooltip that appears wherever the pointer is; large data without expert options; a stated time zone on every instant |\n23:| Highcharts | The best reader experience of the ten | The same class of accessibility with no module to import and no commercial license; reduced-motion support; errors that name the field and the row |\n31:A developer adding charts to a product or an internal dashboard: data arrives as rows from an API, the chart is one component among many on the page, and the person writing it is not a visualization specialist and will not test it with a screen reader. Coding agents writing that code are the same audience: they benefit from typed fields, one logical data model and diagnostics that say what to change.\n40:| Say it, do not guess | Anything Axylo cannot draw faithfully becomes a diagnostic with a code, a location and a fix. If values were left out, the reader is told too. |\n42:| Everything a reader needs is on; nothing else is | Title, units, legend, tooltip, keyboard, description and table are on. Entry animation, a marker on every point of a dense line, and decorative toolbars are not. |\n74:| W6 | Read it another way | Keyboard, screen reader, table view, high contrast, reduced motion, 400% zoom, color-vision deficiency. |\n87:## 3. Acceptance criteria: the reader's experience\n97:| F3 | Input that cannot be plotted as given is reported, never guessed. The diagnostic registry (section 3.5) fixes each case's level, what is drawn and what the reader sees. | One test per code; the combined `dirty` fixture and the four one-bad-value fixtures yield exactly the codes listed in section 3.5 | R5, D5 |\n104:| F10 | Stacks place positive and negative values on their own sides of zero, and the axis covers both totals. The readout's total is the sum when every component has the same sign; when signs are mixed it gives the positive total and the negative total, the two ends the reader sees, not their net. A missing component is drawn with zero height and the stack is marked \"incomplete\" in tooltip, table and announcements. A component is missing whenever its series has no value at that x, whatever the reason: a null, an absent step, or a series that simply has no row there (a weekly series stacked with a daily one is incomplete on the days between its rows; the owner's decision, 2026-11-18: a stack must never show a total made of values the data does not contain). A stacked series' line follows its band, so inside a stack it is not joined across such a slot. | Property test on stacked geometry; mixed-sign and incomplete fixtures | R5 |\n106:| F12 | When a warning means values were left out or changed, the chart says so to the reader: a short note (\"Values not shown: 2. See table.\", worded so it reads correctly for any count) inside the figure and in the accessibility tree, in every build. | Production build given `dirty` shows the note | R5, R6 |\n112:| L1 | No visible text overlaps other text or is clipped, and no label disappears without the reader being told. Remedies, in order: thin (time, date, numeric and `ordered` category axes only); wrap to at most three lines; rotate up to 45°; truncate in the middle, keeping every label distinct, with the full text in tooltip and table. If labels still do not fit, a visible note (\"Some labels hidden. See table.\") appears and AX143 suggests horizontal bars. Axylo never changes a chart's orientation on its own. | In a browser, as painted text and not in a model (in the engines of section 2.3 where results are recorded), after fonts load: for widths 240–1,920 px and generated label sets, zero intersecting text boxes (1 px tolerance), zero text outside the figure, truncated labels pairwise distinct. Repeated with the WCAG 1.4.12 text-spacing overrides, 200% text-only zoom, and a font that loads late | R2, WCAG 1.4.12, 1.4.4 |\n140:| A3 | Moving focus to a point conveys its text (series, x, value with unit, position) through the focused element's value text: the plot is a native range control whose `aria-valuetext` describes the focused point (decision 0001; earlier drafts said \"accessible name or a polite live region\", which the chosen pattern does not use, and A7 forbids announcements for navigation other than this). On touch screen readers, every point is reachable by swiping, or the table is reachable within two gestures. | Accessibility-tree snapshot after each key. No screen-reader rehearsal is part of 1.0 (the owner's decision, 2026-11-18): what a reader actually speaks on NVDA, VoiceOver or TalkBack has not been checked, and the conformance table says so. The script for such a rehearsal is kept in decision 0001 for when one is done. | R12, R13, WCAG 4.1.2 |\n144:| A7 | Data arriving does not speak. Announcements happen only for the reader's own navigation, and for a change of state (L7). While data streams, keyboard focus stays on the same datum until it leaves the window. | Stream test counting live-region changes with and without focus; 24 concurrent streams | R12 |\n151:Level decides delivery. **Error**: the chart or a series cannot be drawn; the chart shows its error state. **Warning**: something is not as the author gave it; where that means values were left out or changed (F12), the reader sees the note, and the registry's rows say which codes those are (a warning about an option, a name, a colour or a tick label shows none). **Hint**: advice for the author. Errors and warnings print once per code per page in every build; hints print in development builds only. `strict: true` throws on errors and warnings found while validating a create, `update` or `append` call, synchronously from that call. A diagnostic that can only arise later (a contrast check after the page's CSS changes, a custom formatter that throws when a table page is built, a label that stops fitting after a resize) is reported when it is found and is never thrown. Every diagnostic names the chart; those about data name the field and the first row indexes in the caller's own data.\n174:| AX143 | Hint | Labels hidden after every remedy | Note shown to the reader |\n182:| AX160 | Warning | A custom formatter threw | The default format is used for that value. A value formatter that throws changes how a value reads, so the reader's note (F12) is shown; a tick formatter that throws only changes an axis label, which is not a value left out or changed, so it is reported without a note (and so the note can never change which ticks fit) |\n217:Automated checks establish structure: names, roles, focus order, contrast, text. They do not establish that a screen-reader user finds a chart understandable. 1.0 has no evidence for that: no screen reader was run against it, not even by the developer (the owner's decision, 2026-11-18; the rehearsal on five reader and browser pairs that earlier drafts of this section planned was not done). Axylo is built by one person with no access to study participants, so no claim of usability testing with disabled users is made, and the conformance table says both things in those words. Reviewers proposed participant testing as a release gate; it is recorded here as a known limit instead.\n234:| DX12 | A coding agent gets the first chart right. Given fixed prompts and trap fixtures (UTC-midnight ISO dates, numeric strings, unsorted rows, missing days, a misspelled field), an agent with only the published docs produces a chart with no error diagnostics that passes the reader criteria. Recorded as agent rehearsal, not as user evidence. | Scripted rehearsal at each release | D1, 1.2 |\n263:| Diagnostics are a first-class feature with codes | R5: none of the ten named the field, the row or the problem. | Kept. Severity and delivery rewritten (3.5): readers are told too, and production builds are not silent. |\n268:| Label fitting is a tested invariant with a visible fallback | R2. | Changed. The absolute \"everything always fits\" was infeasible in a 240 px tile; it is now \"nothing overlaps, and nothing disappears without the reader being told\". Rotation comes before truncation. |\n286:| Test with screen-reader users, outside developers or design partners before release | One-person project with no participants. Recorded as a stated limit (3.8). Agent rehearsal (DX12) is used and labelled as such; the developer's screen-reader rehearsal (A3) was dropped from 1.0 by the owner and is stated as not done. |\n288:| Switch to horizontal bars automatically when labels do not fit | Changing orientation changes what the chart says at a glance; Axylo suggests it and tells the reader what is hidden. |\n289:| Remove the first-chart line-count criterion | Kept as a floor, and joined by DX12, which tests getting it right. |\n298:| Which keyboard and screen-reader pattern for the plot? | Decide from the M1 spike: three candidate patterns on the `trend` chart, rehearsed on the five pairs. | M1 record, confirmed in M4. |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat apps/docs/pages/data.md apps/docs/pages/line.md apps/docs/pages/bar.md apps/docs/pages/getting-started.md apps/docs/pages/diagnostics.md apps/docs/pages/accessibility.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat apps/docs/pages/data.md apps/docs/pages/line.md apps/docs/pages/bar.md apps/docs/pages/getting-started.md apps/docs/pages/diagnostics.md apps/docs/pages/accessibility.md'","aggregated_output":"# Your data\n\nAfter this page you can pass the same table as long rows, wide rows or typed columns; say which kind of time your x values are; control what happens to missing values; and give values a unit, a format and a percent scale.\n\n## One table, three shapes\n\nTake four days with two readings per day: sessions for two channels.\n\n| day | Direct | Referral |\n|---|---|---|\n| 2026-03-02 | 3,120 | 410 |\n| 2026-03-03 | 3,380 | 455 |\n| 2026-03-04 | 3,290 | 390 |\n| 2026-03-05 | 3,510 | 520 |\n\nAxylo accepts this table in three shapes. All three produce the same chart, the same table view and the same diagnostics; row numbers in diagnostics and events refer to your own data in the shape you gave it.\n\n### Long rows\n\nOne row per x and series, as most SQL queries and APIs return it. `series` names the field whose value says which series a row belongs to; that value is the series' key and its name.\n\n[example: data-long](../examples/data-long.ts)\n\n### Wide rows\n\nOne row per x, one field per series. `y` is a list of fields, and there is no `series`. Passing both is a type error, and at run time it raises [AX150](diagnostics.md#ax150). Each field is a series: its key is the field name (`direct`), its name for the reader is the field's `label` (`'Direct'`). Use the key in `colors` and in event handlers, and the label for display. The series share one value axis: the first field's `domain`, `scale` and `tickFormat` set it (a `tickFormat` on a later field is reported, [AX150](diagnostics.md#ax150)), and with more than one field the axis is titled \"Value\" (the `value` message). The chart's generated name and its summary name the series rather than that axis (\"Line chart of Visits and Signups by day\", with fields labelled `'Visits'` and `'Signups'`). When the series have different units, the summary gives each series' range in its own unit and no range for the chart as a whole.\n\n[example: data-wide](../examples/data-wide.ts)\n\n### Typed columns\n\n`data: { columns }` with one array-like per field (an array or a typed array), all of the same length; different lengths are error [AX100](diagnostics.md#ax100). In a `Float64Array` or `Float32Array`, `NaN` means \"no value\" and raises nothing. This shape suits large data, because Axylo can read such a column in place; see [what Axylo reads, keeps and copies](data.md#what-axylo-reads-keeps-and-copies).\n\n[example: data-columns](../examples/data-columns.ts)\n\n## Fields\n\nA field is a key of your rows, written as a string, or an object when it needs more:\n\n```ts\nx: 'day'\ny: { field: 'sessions', label: 'Sessions', unit: 'visits' }\n```\n\nThe object form adds a label (the axis title, and the series name in tooltip, table and announcements), a unit, formats, a scale, a missing-value rule and author bounds. The [options reference](options.md#value-fields) lists them all.\n\nWith TypeScript, field names are checked against your row type. A misspelled field, a text field on a value axis, or wide `y` together with `series` does not compile. With untyped rows (from `fetch().json()`, say) the same mistakes are reported at run time: a field that no row has is [AX101](diagnostics.md#ax101), and the message lists the keys the rows do have and, when one is close, the closest match. With no rows, there is nothing to check a field against, and nothing is reported.\n\n## Three kinds of time\n\nDates are where charts most often go wrong without anyone noticing. Axylo keeps three kinds apart and never mixes them silently.\n\n| Kind | Looks like | Means | Shown |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Calendar date | `'2026-02-24'`, `'2026-02'`, `'2026'` | A day, month or year, wherever you are | The same in every time zone |\n| Instant | Epoch milliseconds with `type: 'time'`, a `Date`, `'2026-02-24T09:30:00Z'`, `'2026-02-24T09:30:00+01:00'` | One moment, the same everywhere | In `timeZone` (default: the viewer's zone), with the zone named in the tooltip heading and the summary |\n| Wall-clock time | `'2026-02-24T09:30'` (no offset) | What a clock showed, zone unknown | As written, with hint [AX112](diagnostics.md#ax112) |\n\n### The off-by-one-day trap\n\nA daily metric is a calendar date. Many back ends send it as an instant at UTC midnight: `'2026-02-24T00:00:00Z'`, or the epoch milliseconds of that moment. For a viewer in New York that moment is the evening of 23 February, so every daily value appears one day early. In Berlin it is still the 24th. Showing the instant in the viewer's zone is correct for an instant; the data said \"this moment\", not \"this day\".\n\nThe example below has the same five values sent both ways. Your browser's time zone decides how the first chart reads: west of UTC, its first point is on the 23rd.\n\n[example: dates](../examples/dates.ts)\n\nAxylo notices at least two distinct instants that all fall at the same time of day, whole days apart, and raises hint [AX111](diagnostics.md#ax111). The fix is one of:\n\n- Send calendar dates: `'2026-02-24'`. `new Date(ms).toISOString().slice(0, 10)` turns a UTC midnight into one.\n- Keep the values and declare `x: { field: 'day', type: 'date' }`. Axylo then reads each instant (string, number or `Date`) as the calendar day it falls on in UTC, as the [daily example](getting-started.md#daily-data-five-things-to-get-right) does.\n- If they really are instants and everyone should see UTC, set `x: { field: 'day', type: 'time', timeZone: 'UTC' }`.\n\n### How Axylo decides\n\nWhen `type` is not given, each value of the x field votes for a kind:\n\n- `YYYY-MM-DD`, `YYYY-MM` or `YYYY` strings for a calendar date;\n- date-time strings with `Z` or an offset, and `Date` objects, for an instant;\n- date-time strings without an offset for a wall-clock time;\n- finite numbers for a number. A bare number is never taken for an instant: epoch milliseconds need `type: 'time'`, and numbers that look like timestamps raise hint [AX109](diagnostics.md#ax109);\n- anything else for a category.\n\nCategories win when they are at least as many as all the other votes together. Otherwise the kind with the most votes wins, ties going to date, then instant, then wall-clock time, then number. Values of any other kind are then unusable: they are not drawn, and they raise [AX107](diagnostics.md#ax107) with a note to the reader. Three category strings, two dates and two numbers therefore make a date axis that keeps the two dates. When a feed mixes kinds, declare `type`.\n\nCategories keep the order in which they first appear.\n\n`timeZone` takes `'local'` (the default, the viewer's zone), `'UTC'`, or an IANA zone such as `'Europe/Paris'`. It applies to instants. A zone the browser does not know is [AX152](diagnostics.md#ax152), and the viewer's zone is used.\n\nCalendar periods other than years and months (quarters, ISO weeks) are categories in 1.0: write them as you want them read (`'2025 Q1'`) and set `ordered: true` on the x field so that their labels may be thinned when they do not fit.\n\n## Missing values\n\nAxylo never draws a missing value as zero unless you ask, and does not connect a line across a missing value it knows about. It knows about one when your data says so:\n\n| In your data | Drawn | Reported |\n|---|---|---|\n| `null`, or `''` | A gap | Nothing: this is how to say \"no data\" |\n| `NaN` in a `Float64Array` or `Float32Array` | A gap | Nothing |\n| The value field absent from a row (`undefined`) | A gap | Warning [AX102](diagnostics.md#ax102), and the reader's note |\n| The x or series field absent from a row | The row is not drawn | Warning [AX102](diagnostics.md#ax102), and the reader's note |\n| `NaN` or `Infinity` in rows | A gap | Warning [AX103](diagnostics.md#ax103), and the reader's note |\n| `0` | Zero | Nothing |\n\nA day with **no row at all** is different: the data says nothing about it. Axylo infers that a step is missing only from a series' own rows, when the x axis is dates, instants, wall-clock times or numbers and the series is regular: it has at least three rows, every interval between neighbouring rows is a whole multiple of the smallest one, and at least half of the intervals are the smallest one. A missing step it infers is drawn as a gap, as if the row held `null`, with hint [AX105](diagnostics.md#ax105). It infers nothing before the first row or after the last, in a series of one or two rows, in an irregular series, or when there would be more than twice as many missing steps as rows (and more than 1,000). Another series having a row at that x does not make the step missing in this one: a slot a series simply has no row for (a weekly series beside a daily one) is not a gap, its line is joined across it, and a bar chart draws no bar and no \"no data\" mark there. In a stack it is different, see below. So **when you know a day is missing, send it with `null`**: that is the instruction Axylo always follows.\n\nA value standing alone between two gaps is drawn as a point, so it does not vanish. On a bar chart a missing value gets a small \"no data\" mark (at the baseline, or for a stacked bar at the edge of its stack), distinct from a zero bar. Tooltip, table and announcements say \"No data\". When a warning means values were left out, the chart shows a short note to the reader (\"Values not shown: 3. See table.\") inside the figure, in every build.\n\n**In a stack** (stacked areas or bars), a series with no value at an x is a missing part, whatever the reason: a `null`, an inferred missing step, or no row there at all. The part has zero height, the series' line follows its band, and the total at that x is marked \"incomplete\" in the tooltip, the table and announcements, so a stack never shows a total made of values the data does not contain. A weekly series stacked with a daily one is incomplete on the days between its rows; see [the area chart's example](area.md#edge-case-a-missing-part-of-a-stack).\n\nFor counts, where a day without a row means nothing happened, declare it: `missing: 'zero'` turns null, absent and inferred-missing values of that field into zeros, and the note says the values are shown differently from the data.\n\n[example: missing](../examples/missing.ts)\n\nHere are the values an application sends by mistake, all at once. The note under the chart is what the reader sees; the console has one line per code.\n\n[example: line-dirty](../examples/line-dirty.ts)\n\n## Numbers sent as strings\n\nSome database drivers return counts and decimals as strings (Postgres `bigint` and `numeric`, BigQuery). Axylo looks at the whole field:\n\n- When every value is a plain decimal string (`'40'`, `'-1.5'`, and exponent notation such as `'1e3'`), the field is converted and drawn, and hint [AX104](diagnostics.md#ax104) asks you to say so with `parse: 'number'`. With that, there is no hint, and TypeScript accepts the string field on a value axis.\n- When the field also holds a number, or any string that is not a plain decimal (`'1,234'`, `'n/a'`), or another kind of value (`true`), every string in it is rejected, plain decimals included. They are not drawn, and they are warning [AX106](diagnostics.md#ax106).\n\nThe conversion is to JavaScript numbers. Integers are exact up to 9,007,199,254,740,991 (`Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER`); a larger one is rounded to the nearest number JavaScript can hold, without a diagnostic: `'9007199254740993'` reads 9,007,199,254,740,992. A string too large for a number (`'1e999'`) becomes infinite and is [AX103](diagnostics.md#ax103). When exact counts could exceed that limit, check them with `Number.isSafeInteger(Number(value))` before charting.\n\n[example: numeric-strings](../examples/numeric-strings.ts)\n\n## Order and duplicates\n\nRows can come in any order. Line, area and scatter charts are drawn in x order, and each value stays with its own row; categories keep the order in which they first appear.\n\n[example: unsorted](../examples/unsorted.ts)\n\nOn a line, area or bar chart one x has one value per series. Two rows with the same x in the same series are error [AX108](diagnostics.md#ax108): that series is not drawn, while the other series are; with no series left to draw, the chart shows its error state and keeps its table. Axylo does not know whether you meant the sum, the last or the average. Two donut slices with the same label are the same error, and the donut is not drawn. On a scatter chart repeated x values, and repeated points, are ordinary data.\n\n## Units, formats and percentages\n\n| Option on a value field | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| `label` | The axis title, and the series name in tooltip, table and announcements. Default: the field name. |\n| `unit` | Follows every value in tooltip, table and announcements, and is part of the axis title. |\n| `format` | How values are written in tooltip, table and announcements: `Intl.NumberFormat` options, or a function. Default: the locale's grouping, the full integer part and up to six significant fraction digits (`123,456.75`). |\n| `tickFormat` | How axis ticks are written. Default: the locale's grouping with the fraction digits the tick step needs (`50,000`); compact (`1.2M`) when the largest tick is at least a million; scientific from 10¹⁵. |\n| `scale: 'percent'` | The values are percentages from 0 to 100. |\n| `scale: 'ratio'` | The values are fractions from 0 to 1, shown as percentages. |\n\nA percent or ratio scale is declared, never guessed from a label. Its axis ends at full scale (100%) only when the largest value is at least half of full scale and not above it; otherwise it ends at the first tick at or above the data, so 4% is not drawn as a sliver of 100%. Numbers and dates are formatted for the `locale` option if given, else the document's language (`<html lang>`), else the browser's. A formatter that fails (a function that throws, or `Intl` options that `Intl` rejects) is warning [AX160](diagnostics.md#ax160), and the default format is used for that value.\n\n[example: units](../examples/units.ts)\n\nBar and area value axes always include zero, since a bar's length and an area's height are their values. A `domain` that leaves zero out is widened, with warning [AX110](diagnostics.md#ax110). Line and scatter axes fit the data.\n\nValue axes (y, and both axes of a scatter chart) end on a drawn tick at or beyond the data, the reference lines and bands, and stack totals; in a plot too narrow to label an end tick, the axis takes the next coarser step. A date, time or number x axis of a line, area or bar chart spans exactly its data instead (for bars, plus half a band at each end), so a time series is not padded to the next round tick.\n\n## What Axylo reads, keeps and copies\n\n- **Rows** are read when the chart is created or updated, and never changed. Their values are copied into Axylo's own typed arrays. Events hand back your own row object (`point.row`), and diagnostics name rows by their index in your data. A position no row of yours holds (an inferred missing day, a donut's \"Other\") has `row: null` and `index: -1`; check before you look a row up.\n- **Typed columns** of values (`Float64Array`, `Float32Array`) are read in place, without a copy, when Axylo can: the x values are already in order, no missing step is inferred, and no value has to change (no `Infinity`, no `missing: 'zero'` to apply). Otherwise Axylo copies what it needs; grouping long columns into series and scatter charts can need new storage too. Either way, do not change an array after passing it: to change the data, pass new arrays with `update({ data })`.\n- **Appended rows** are read once and added to the chart's own storage; `keep` limits how much is kept. See [Updating, streaming and zoom](updating.md#append-and-keep).\n\n## Checking data before you draw\n\n`validate(type, options)` runs the same reading and checking as a chart, without a page, and returns the diagnostics. Use it in Node, in a unit test, or in a coding agent's loop before rendering. See [Diagnostics](diagnostics.md#validate-in-node-and-in-tests).\n# Line charts\n\nAfter this page you know when to use a line chart, which options it takes, and how a line is picked, read by keyboard, zoomed and simplified.\n\n## When to use it\n\nA line shows how a measure changed along an ordered x: sessions per day, latency per minute, revenue per month. Several series compare trends. The x can be calendar dates, instants, wall-clock times, numbers or categories.\n\nUse a [bar chart](bar.md) to compare a few categories, an [area chart](area.md) when the total and its parts matter, and a [compact line](compact.md) for a sparkline in a KPI tile.\n\n[example: line-time](../examples/line-time.ts)\n\n## Options\n\n```ts\nimport { line } from 'axylo';\nline(target, { data, x, y, series, ...options });\n```\n\n`target` is a CSS selector or an `HTMLElement`; a selector that matches nothing throws an error that names it.\n\n| Option | On a line chart |\n|---|---|\n| `x` | Dates, instants, wall-clock times, numbers or categories. See [the three kinds of time](data.md#three-kinds-of-time). |\n| `y` | One value field (with `series` for long rows, or alone for one series), or a list of fields for wide rows. |\n| `series` | For long rows only: the field that names each row's series. A single `y` needs no `series`. |\n| `zoom` | On by default for date, time and number x. `'touch'` also takes pinch and two-finger pan. |\n| `reference` | Lines and bands: `{ y: 500, label: 'Objective' }`, `{ x: [from, to], label: 'Incident' }`. |\n| `compact` | Marks only, for a tile. See [Compact](compact.md). |\n| Common options | `title`, `labelledBy`, `subtitle`, `description`, `state`, `legend`, `tooltip`, `table`, `colors`, `messages`, `locale`, `strict`, `onDiagnostic`. |\n\nNot on a line chart, and a type error if given: `stacked` (stacking lines hides how each part moves; a stacked area shows it), `layout` and `horizontal` (bars only), `label` and `value` (donut only). At run time an option that does not apply is ignored with [AX150](diagnostics.md#ax150). The [options reference](options.md) has every option with its type and default.\n\n## How a line chart behaves\n\n| Behaviour | Line |\n|---|---|\n| Pointer | The nearest x is picked, wherever the pointer is in the plot. The tooltip, placed against the marks at that x, lists every visible series there (up to eight rows, then \"+N more\"). A series with no row at that x reads \"No observation\"; one whose value is missing reads \"No data\". A click pins the tooltip. In a plot smaller than 240 × 140 px the same text is written in a strip under the plot; see [reading values with a pointer](accessibility.md#reading-values-with-a-pointer). |\n| Keyboard | By x, then series. Left and Right move along x with every visible series at once; Up and Down narrow to one series and move between series; Escape returns to all series. |\n| Zoom | Date, time and number x: the Zoom in and Zoom out buttons (Pan left, Pan right and Reset zoom once zoomed), dragging across the plot or two clicks with Select range, the keys `+` `-` `0` and Shift with the arrows, the wheel while the plot has focus, and pinch with `zoom: 'touch'`. A category axis and a compact chart do not zoom. See [zoom](updating.md#zoom). |\n| Many points | When a line is denser than the screen, the drawing is simplified: in every device-pixel column, each run of valid samples keeps its minimum and maximum, and a break between runs is kept. Nothing is smoothed. Tooltip, keyboard and table read the data, not the drawing, so every sample stays reachable. |\n| Markers | Each series carries its marker shape at its last point and at sparse intervals, repeated in the legend and tooltip, so a dense line still has some. Every point gets a marker only when neighbouring points are at least 12 px apart. A point alone between gaps always has one. |\n| Gaps | A missing value you send (`null`) is a gap. A missing step with no row is a gap only when Axylo can infer it from the series (see [missing values](data.md#missing-values)). |\n| Many series | Eight series have eight colours. From the ninth, colours repeat and each series keeps its own combination of colour and dash or marker, with warning [AX130](diagnostics.md#ax130). |\n| Order and duplicates | Drawn in x order whatever the input order. Two rows for the same x in one series are [AX108](diagnostics.md#ax108): that series is not drawn, the others are. |\n| Reference lines and bands | Drawn and labelled, included in the automatic value domain, and listed in the summary and the table caption. An author `domain` that leaves a reference out keeps it out of the drawing; the summary still lists it. |\n\nThe value axis of a line fits the data and need not start at zero, unlike a bar or area.\n\n## Many points\n\nA hundred thousand readings, drawn simplified and read exactly: press End on the plot to reach the last reading, or open the table.\n\n[example: line-dense](../examples/line-dense.ts)\n\n## More than eight series\n\n[example: line-many-series](../examples/line-many-series.ts)\n\n## Edge case: values that cannot be drawn\n\nNull is a gap without comment. `NaN`, a stray string and an absent field are gaps too, each with its own diagnostic, and the reader is told how many values are not shown. A value alone between gaps is a point.\n\n[example: line-dirty](../examples/line-dirty.ts)\n# Bar charts\n\nAfter this page you know when to use a bar chart, how to group, stack and turn bars horizontal, and what Axylo does with long labels, missing bars and negative values.\n\n## When to use it\n\nBars compare categories: satisfaction by team, tickets by product area, orders per region. A bar's length is its value, so the value axis always includes zero. Bars over a date axis compare counts per period (orders per day).\n\nUse a [line chart](line.md) to show a trend over many points, and a [donut](donut.md) only for the shares of one whole.\n\n[example: bar-target](../examples/bar-target.ts)\n\n## Options\n\n```ts\nimport { bar } from 'axylo';\nbar(target, { data, x, y, series, layout: 'grouped', horizontal: false, ...options });\n```\n\n| Option | On a bar chart |\n|---|---|\n| `x` | The categories (or dates, numbers). Categories keep the order of the data. `ordered: true` says they have a natural order (months, age bands), which allows thinning their labels. |\n| `y`, `series` | One value field (with `series` for long rows), or a list of fields. Several series make groups. |\n| `layout` | `'grouped'` (default) puts the series side by side; `'stacked'` stacks them. |\n| `horizontal` | `true` draws the bars from the left, with the categories down the side. Default: `false`. A `y` reference then runs up and down the plot, and an `x` reference across it. |\n| `zoom` | On date, time and number x only. A category axis does not zoom. |\n| `reference`, `compact` | As on a line chart. |\n| `domain` on `y` | Widened to include zero, with [AX110](diagnostics.md#ax110). |\n| Common options | `title`, `labelledBy`, `subtitle`, `description`, `state`, `legend`, `tooltip`, `table`, `colors`, `messages`, `locale`, `strict`, `onDiagnostic`. |\n\nNot on a bar chart: `stacked` (that is `layout: 'stacked'` here), `label` and `value` (donut only).\n\n## How a bar chart behaves\n\n| Behaviour | Bar |\n|---|---|\n| Pointer | The band under the pointer: the whole category, not only the bar. The tooltip lists every series in that category; a click pins it. See [reading values with a pointer](accessibility.md#reading-values-with-a-pointer). |\n| Keyboard | By category, then series. |\n| Zoom | Only on date, time and number x, as on a line. On a category axis `zoom()` changes nothing and is reported ([AX150](diagnostics.md#ax150)). |\n| Baseline | The value axis always includes zero; an author `domain` without zero is widened ([AX110](diagnostics.md#ax110)). Grouped bars grow from zero. |\n| Missing values | A missing bar (a `null` or absent value, or a step Axylo infers is missing) gets a small \"no data\" mark, distinct from a zero bar: at the baseline for grouped bars, at the edge of the stack for stacked ones. A slot a series simply has no row for, as for a weekly series beside daily ones, gets no bar and no mark. |\n| Stacking | Positive parts stack up from zero, negative parts down from it, and the axis covers both. With mixed signs each bar shows two totals, of its positive and of its negative parts, and the readout names both (\"Total 165 k USD and -60 k USD\" in the example below); the bar does not show their difference. A missing part has zero height and the total is marked \"incomplete\"; a part is missing whenever its series has no value at that x, also when the series simply has no row there. |\n| Long labels | Remedies in order: thinning (date, number and `ordered` category axes only), wrapping to three lines, rotating up to 45°, truncating in the middle with the full text in tooltip and table. If labels still do not fit, the chart says \"Some labels hidden. See table.\" and [AX143](diagnostics.md#ax143) suggests `horizontal: true`. Axylo never switches orientation by itself: that would change what the chart says at a glance. |\n| Many bars | Not simplified. More x positions than pixels along the axis are drawn, with hint [AX132](diagnostics.md#ax132). |\n| Many series | Colour and fill pattern: from the ninth series bars get patterns, which gives 32 distinct combinations before they repeat; warning [AX130](diagnostics.md#ax130) from the ninth. A [compact](compact.md) bar chart, which has no legend, gives neighbouring series different patterns from the second series on. |\n| Reference lines | Drawn and labelled, included in the automatic domain, listed in the summary and table caption. |\n\n## Horizontal and grouped bars\n\nLong category names read best down the side. With two value fields the bars are grouped, and a missing value is a \"no data\" mark, not an empty slot that looks like zero.\n\n[example: bar-horizontal](../examples/bar-horizontal.ts)\n\n## Edge case: stacks with both signs\n\n[example: bar-stacked](../examples/bar-stacked.ts)\n# Getting started\n\nAfter this page you can install Axylo, draw a first chart from rows with npm or a script tag, use it inside a framework, get the five things right that most often make a daily chart wrong, and know what to check when a chart looks wrong.\n\n## Install\n\nWith npm and a bundler:\n\n```sh\nnpm install axylo\n```\n\nWith a script tag, load one file from the package's `dist` folder: `axylo.iife.js` is the development build (it prints hints), `axylo.iife.min.js` the production build. Both define one global, `Axylo`.\n\nThe package has no runtime dependencies. Its module build comes in two flavours, chosen by the `development` export condition: a bundler that resolves that condition gets the development build, which also prints hints; otherwise you get the production build, which prints errors and warnings only. The styles need no import: the library adopts its stylesheet as a constructed style sheet, or, where that is not possible, adds a `<style>` element. A page that prefers to load the stylesheet itself can link `axylo/axylo.css`, and the library then uses that copy. The packed packages are tested in Chrome under a Content Security Policy without `unsafe-inline`, with the stylesheet adopted and with it linked (`tests/dist/csp.browser.test.ts`).\n\nTypeScript 5.4 or later gives you checked field names. The package's declarations refer to DOM types (`HTMLElement`), so a TypeScript project needs the `DOM` library in `lib`, also for code that only calls `validate()` in Node. Plain JavaScript works the same without types.\n\n## The first chart\n\nEach of these runs live below its source. They are kept in the repository as the files the package's distribution tests install and render (`examples/first-chart`), and each says what page it needs around it.\n\nWith npm and a bundler:\n\n[example: first-chart-npm](../../../examples/first-chart/npm.ts)\n\nWith a script tag:\n\n[example: first-chart-script](../../../examples/first-chart/script.html)\n\nPoint at a chart, or press Tab to reach its plot and use the arrow keys, or open its table. The only required options are `data` and the fields: `x` and `y` (and `series` for long rows), or `label` and `value` for a donut. `title` is not required, but without it (or `labelledBy`) the chart gets a generated name and warning [AX140](diagnostics.md#ax140).\n\n## Using Axylo in a framework\n\nNo framework adapter ships in 1.0; adapters for React, Vue, Svelte and others are planned after 1.0. Axylo is called from any framework's own hooks: create the chart when its element exists, call `update()` when your data or options change, and call `destroy()` when the element goes away. Creating, destroying and creating again on the same element is supported; it is the sequence a framework's development mode produces.\n\nThe lifecycle as three plain functions:\n\n[example: lifecycle](../examples/lifecycle.ts)\n\n## Daily data: five things to get right\n\nThe first chart above has months as calendar dates and numbers as numbers. Data from an API is often less tidy. Here is a daily metric as many back ends send it, and the chart that reads it correctly:\n\n[example: daily-sessions](../examples/daily-sessions.ts)\n\n1. **Days sent as UTC midnights.** For daily totals sent as `'2026-02-24T00:00:00Z'` (or as epoch milliseconds of a UTC midnight), use `x: { field: 'day', type: 'date' }`. Without it they are instants, shown in the viewer's time zone, so west of UTC each value appears on the day before, with only hint [AX111](diagnostics.md#ax111). Strings such as `'2026-02-24'` need no `type`. See [the three kinds of time](data.md#three-kinds-of-time).\n2. **Numbers sent as strings.** For decimal strings, use `y: { field: 'sessions', parse: 'number' }`. They are converted to JavaScript numbers, which are exact for integers up to 9,007,199,254,740,991; a larger count is rounded without a warning, so check exact counts with `Number.isSafeInteger` first. A field that mixes numbers and strings is not converted at all. See [numbers sent as strings](data.md#numbers-sent-as-strings).\n3. **Row order.** Pass rows in any order. Axylo sorts dated rows by x and keeps each value with its own row; categories keep the order in which they first appear.\n4. **A day with no data.** When you know a day is missing, send it, with `sessions: null`. Axylo can infer a missing day only between two rows of a series that has at least three rows at a regular step, and never before the first row or after the last; with two rows, or an irregular series, the line runs straight across the missing day. See [missing values](data.md#missing-values).\n5. **A misspelled field.** On error [AX101](diagnostics.md#ax101), compare the field you named with the keys the message lists, which come from your rows, and use the suggested spelling when one is offered. With no rows at all the keys cannot be checked, so the error appears once data arrives. With typed rows in TypeScript the typo does not compile.\n\n## What you get without asking\n\n| Feature | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| Name and summary | `title` is the visible heading and the chart's accessible name. In a card that already has a heading, `labelledBy` names the chart with it. The chart's description is a generated summary: chart type, series, the x range with its time zone, the value range, each series' minimum, maximum and last value, and anything left out. `description` adds to it. |\n| Table view | \"View as table\" shows the data as an HTML table, 100 rows per page; above 100 rows a pager and a \"Jump to\" field appear. |\n| Keyboard | Up to two tab stops while the table is closed: the plot, where the arrow keys move through the points and its value text describes the point in focus, and the toolbar, when the chart has controls. A compact chart has none. See [the keyboard map](accessibility.md#the-keyboard-map). |\n| Reading values | Pointing in the plot shows what is there, by the chart type's rule (nearest x for line and area, the band for bars, the nearest point within 24 px for scatter, the slice for a donut). The tooltip sits against the marks it describes, not at the pointer, and lets the pointer through; rest on a point to move onto its tooltip, click to pin it. In a small plot the same text goes in a strip under the plot. Escape closes it. See [reading values with a pointer](accessibility.md#reading-values-with-a-pointer). |\n| Colour and more than colour | Eight default colours that work in light and dark pages. Lines and areas also carry marker shapes, repeated in the legend and tooltip; bars take fill patterns past the eighth series. |\n| Page theme | Font, text colour and light or dark come from the page. The chart repaints when the page switches scheme. |\n| Size | The chart fills its container's width. With no height set, the plot is 16:9 of the width, between 160 and 480 px tall. It follows the container as it resizes, with no call from you. |\n| States | No rows shows \"No data to show.\" inside the plot, never an empty frame or an invented axis. `state` sets loading, empty and error messages of your own. |\n| Diagnostics | Anything that cannot be drawn as given is reported with a code, the field and rows where they apply, and the fix. When values were left out or changed, the reader is told too. |\n\nThe four states, each a chart with no rows:\n\n[example: states](../examples/states.ts)\n\n## When it looks wrong: three things to check\n\n**1. Read the diagnostics.** Open the browser console. Every problem Axylo found is printed once per code per page as `[axylo AX…] \"Chart title\": what is wrong, where, and how to fix it`. The same list is on the chart as `chart.diagnostics`, current as soon as the call returns. Each code has a page under [Diagnostics](diagnostics.md). Hints, which are advice rather than problems, print in the development build only.\n\n**2. Check which kind of x Axylo read.** A date off by one day almost always means instants where calendar dates were meant (item 1 above). If the tooltip heading names a time zone, Axylo read instants. If the x labels are your strings in input order, it read categories.\n\n**3. Check the values without drawing.** `validate()` runs the same checks as creating the chart, in Node or in a test, with the same options as the chart:\n\n```ts\nimport { validate } from 'axylo';\n\nconst problems = validate('line', { title: 'Monthly visitors', data: rows, x: 'month', y: 'visitors' });\nfor (const d of problems) console.log(d.code, d.level, d.message);\n```\n\nNumbers that arrive as strings, a missing day, two rows for the same day and a misspelled field each have their own code; [Your data](data.md) covers each case.\n\n## Next\n\nRead [Your data](data.md) next: it explains the three shapes rows can take, how dates are read, and what happens to missing values.\n# Diagnostics\n\nAfter this page you can read an Axylo diagnostic, collect diagnostics in your own code, make a chart throw on them, and check a chart's data in Node or in a test before anything is drawn. Every code has its own page, listed at the end.\n\n## Reading one\n\nWhen Axylo cannot draw something as given, it prints one line to the console:\n\n[output: AX103](../data/diagnostics.mjs)\n\n| Part | Here |\n|---|---|\n| Code | `AX103`. Stable across releases; each has a page. |\n| Chart | `\"Conversion\"`: the chart's title, or its element id when it has none. |\n| What and where | The field, how many values, and the first rows (up to ten), numbered in your own data as you passed it. A diagnostic about the chart as a whole (no name, too many series, low contrast) has no field and no rows. |\n| Fix | What to change. |\n| Outcome | What the chart did instead: here, gaps. |\n\n## Levels and who is told\n\n| Level | Means | Console |\n|---|---|---|\n| Error | Something cannot be drawn as given: the chart, a series, or the donut. | Every build, once per code per page, with `console.warn` |\n| Warning | Something is not drawn as given, or an option is ignored. | Every build, once per code per page, with `console.warn` |\n| Hint | Advice for you. | Development builds only, with `console.info` |\n\nThe level says who is told; it does not by itself say what stays on screen. That depends on the code, and each code's page says it, with a checked minimal input. For example: an error about one series ([AX108](diagnostics.md#ax108)) leaves the other series drawn; a warning can put the chart in its error state when the ignored option was needed to draw at all (a `y` list on a scatter chart, [AX150](diagnostics.md#ax150)); and some hints come with a visible change: an inferred missing step is drawn as a gap ([AX105](diagnostics.md#ax105)), a plot too small to draw is replaced by its summary ([AX142](diagnostics.md#ax142)), hidden labels get a note ([AX143](diagnostics.md#ax143)).\n\nWhen values were left out or changed, the chart shows the reader a note in the figure (\"Values not shown: 3. See table.\", or \"Values shown differently from the data\"), in every build, and in the accessibility tree.\n\n## Collecting them in your code\n\nEach diagnostic is an object:\n\n```ts\ninterface Diagnostic {\n  code: `AX${number}`;           // 'AX103'\n  level: 'error' | 'warning' | 'hint';\n  message: string;               // what is wrong, where, and how to fix it\n  chart: string;                 // the chart's title, else its element id\n  field?: string;                // when it is about a field\n  rows?: readonly number[];      // first rows, in your data, at most 10\n  count?: number;                // how many values or rows in all\n}\n```\n\n`chart.diagnostics` lists them for the current options and data, up to date as soon as a create, `update` or `append` returns. `onDiagnostic` is called once for each diagnostic the chart newly finds, in every build and at every level, so it is the place to send them to your error tracking. A listener that throws is reported once and otherwise ignored; it cannot break the chart.\n\n[example: diagnostics-listener](../examples/diagnostics-listener.html)\n\n## When they are found\n\nMost diagnostics are found while a call checks its input: the create, `update` or `append` call. Those are in `chart.diagnostics` when the call returns. A few can only be found later, while drawing: low contrast after the page's CSS changes ([AX141](diagnostics.md#ax141)), a plot too small ([AX142](diagnostics.md#ax142)), labels that stop fitting after a resize ([AX143](diagnostics.md#ax143)), more bars than pixels ([AX132](diagnostics.md#ax132)), a formatter that fails ([AX160](diagnostics.md#ax160)), a `labelledBy` that names no element when there is no `title` ([AX140](diagnostics.md#ax140)), and an internal failure ([AX199](diagnostics.md#ax199)). They are reported when found, through the same console line and `onDiagnostic`. A `zoom()` call that cannot apply (a category axis, a donut, a compact chart) is reported when it is made, through the console line and `onDiagnostic` only: it changes nothing, so `chart.diagnostics` does not list it ([AX150](diagnostics.md#ax150)).\n\n## Strict mode\n\n`strict: true` makes the chart throw on any error or warning found while checking a create, `update` or `append` call, synchronously from that call. The chart keeps its last valid state; a rejected create leaves nothing in the page. The error is named `AxyloStrictError`, and its `diagnostics` property has every diagnostic of the rejected call. Hints never throw, and neither do diagnostics found later while drawing. A failure while a call is being checked (such as a row getter that throws, [AX199](diagnostics.md#ax199)) rejects that call too, with AX199 after what it had found; a failure later, while drawing, is never thrown. Each code's page says whether a strict chart throws it. See the example in [Updating](updating.md#strict-mode).\n\n## validate() in Node and in tests\n\n`validate(type, options)` runs the same reading and checking as creating a chart (options, data, view, name) without a DOM, and returns the diagnostics a created chart would report from its call. It takes the same options as the chart type, so it type-checks the same way. Importing `axylo` in Node does not touch the DOM.\n\nUse it on a server, in a build step, or in a coding agent's loop:\n\n[source: examples/node/validate.ts](../examples/node/validate.ts)\n\nOr in a unit test, so that a change in the data a query returns fails the build instead of a dashboard:\n\n[source: examples/node/chart-data.test.ts](../examples/node/chart-data.test.ts)\n\nBoth files are type-checked and run by this documentation's own tests, against the packed package. The package's declarations refer to DOM types, so a TypeScript configuration for Node code needs `\"lib\": [\"ES2022\", \"DOM\"]` (or `skipLibCheck`). `validate()` cannot find what only a page can show: the diagnostics listed under \"When they are found\" need a browser. It can tell that a chart has neither `title` nor `labelledBy`, but not whether a `labelledBy` id exists.\n\n## Every code\n\n[table: diagnostic codes](../data/diagnostics.mjs)\n# Accessibility\n\nAfter this page you know what an Axylo chart exposes to assistive technology, to the keyboard and to readers with low vision, high-contrast settings or a colour-vision deficiency; which keys do what; how values are read with a pointer; what you as the author must supply; and what has and has not been verified.\n\nThese features are on by default: there is no module to import and no option to turn on.\n\n## What the chart provides\n\n| For | What the chart provides |\n|---|---|\n| Assistive technology | The figure is a group with an accessible name (`title`, or the heading named by `labelledBy`, or a generated name) and a description: the generated summary of type, series, x range with its time zone, value range, each series' minimum, maximum and last value, and anything left out. A compact chart is an image with the same name and description. The plot is one native range input whose value text describes the point in focus: its x, the value of each visible series with its unit, and its position (\"Point 4 of 120\"). Changes of state (loading, empty, error) are written once to a polite live region. \"View as table\" opens the data as an HTML table with a caption, column headers with units, and row headers. |\n| Keyboard | Up to two tab stops while the table is closed: the plot, and the toolbar when the chart has controls. A compact chart has none. Every point is reachable in order, the series can be switched, the ends are one key away, and Tab leaves the chart. The focus indicator is at least 2 px wide, in the focus colour, which meets 3:1 on the default backgrounds; it always shows while you use the keyboard in the plot, and it may also show after a click. |\n| Low vision | Text is HTML at 12 px or more by default, with 4.5:1 contrast for text and 3:1 for marks, axes and focus indicators in the default colours. Text follows browser zoom and the page's text-spacing settings. Labels never overlap; when one cannot be shown, the chart says so. The tooltip is placed against the marks it describes, not at the pointer, and does not cover them, a control or the focus ring; in a small plot its content is written in a strip under the plot instead (see [Reading values with a pointer](#reading-values-with-a-pointer)). |\n| Colour-vision deficiency | Eight colours chosen to stay apart under simulated protanopia, deuteranopia and tritanopia, and never colour alone: lines and areas carry marker shapes, repeated in the legend and tooltip; bars by position and order, named in the legend and tooltip, and also by fill pattern past the eighth series and between neighbours in a compact chart, which has no legend; adjacent fills are separated by background. |\n| High contrast | In forced-colours mode the chart uses system colours, and lines also differ by dash; so they do under `prefers-contrast: more`. |\n| Reduced motion | Nothing is animated on first render. Under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` nothing transitions at all. |\n| Touch and a single pointer | Every control is at least 24 × 24 px. Everything a drag does can be done with single clicks or taps: Zoom in and out, Pan left and right, and Select range for choosing a range with two clicks. A one-finger drag scrolls the page. |\n\nThe legend entries of a line, area, bar or scatter chart are toggle buttons with a pressed state; a hidden series keeps its full-contrast text, struck through. A chart with one series has no legend entries. A donut's legend lists every slice, each with a button that selects that slice (the chart fires `select`), without a pressed state.\n\n## The keyboard map\n\nThe plot is a single native range input laid over the chart. Its keys:\n\n| Key | In the plot |\n|---|---|\n| Left, Right | Previous or next x, with every visible series there. Scatter: the previous or next point in x, then y order. Donut: the previous or next slice. |\n| Home, End | The first or last point. |\n| Page Up, Page Down | Forward or back by one tenth of the number of points, rounded down, and at least one. |\n| Up, Down | Narrow to one series and move to the previous or next series. Donut: the next or previous slice. |\n| Escape | One thing per press, in this order: end a drag; release Select range; close the tooltip or end a pin: the datum on show stays closed until the pointer has reached a different datum, and nothing else opens at once (after Escape from a held tooltip, the datum under the pointer stays closed too); return from one series to all. When none of these applies, the key is left to the page, so a dialog around the chart closes only on an Escape the chart did not use. |\n| Enter, Space | Select the point: the chart fires its `select` event. |\n| `+`, `-`, `0` | Zoom in, zoom out, show everything (date, time and number x). |\n| Shift with Left or Right | Pan the zoomed view. |\n| Wheel | Zoom, only while the plot has focus and never with Ctrl or Meta held. |\n\nThe toolbar holds the controls that apply to the chart: legend toggles, Zoom in, Select range, Zoom out, Pan left, Pan right, Reset zoom, Pause, View as table. It is one tab stop. Inside it the arrow keys move between controls, Home and End go to the first and last, and Enter or Space presses the control. The open table adds the table's scroll region to the tab order, and, when there are more than 100 rows, its pager (previous page, next page and a \"Jump to\" field). The plot's accessible description gives a short version of this map.\n\nWith several charts on a page, an Escape belongs to the chart that has focus or, when focus is in none of them, to the one that showed something last; one press is used by one chart at most.\n\nThis pattern was chosen by reasoning and by inspecting the browsers' accessibility trees, not by testing with screen readers; see [decision 0001](../../../docs/decisions/0001-plot-interaction-pattern.md) and [decision 0004](../../../docs/decisions/0004-tooltip-and-focused-text.md).\n\n## Reading values with a pointer\n\nPointing anywhere in the plot shows what is there, by the chart type's rule: on line and area charts the nearest x, on bars the band under the pointer, on scatter charts the nearest point within 24 px, on a donut the slice under the pointer. What is shown depends on the room the plot has:\n\n| Plot | What pointing shows |\n|---|---|\n| At least 240 × 140 px | A floating tooltip. |\n| Narrower than 240 px or shorter than 140 px | The same content, written as running text (\"x: series value; series value; +2 more\") in a strip of two lines reserved under the plot. The strip covers nothing, and nothing covers it. |\n| So small that the strip would leave the plot under 80 px high | No readout. The datum is highlighted; its values are in the plot's keyboard reading and in the table. |\n\nThe choice is made by layout, when the chart is laid out, never by the pointer.\n\n**Where the tooltip goes.** It is placed against the marks of the datum it describes, never at the pointer: above them first, then below, then to the right or the left (for horizontal bars: right, left, above, below); a donut's tooltip goes in its hole first, when it fits there. It stays inside the plot, the viewport and any container that clips the chart, and covers neither those marks, a control nor the focus ring; rows that do not fit are counted in \"+N more\". Only when not even one row fits anywhere may it partly cover the marks, never a control or the focus ring, and the table still has every value.\n\n**Long text.** A value that fits stays on one line with its unit; a longer one wraps inside the tooltip, which is never wider or taller than the visible part of the plot. However long the text, the tooltip shows its heading and at least one row, cut if they must be; it is left out only when every place for it would cover a control or the focus ring. In the strip, entries wrap within its two lines, and when not even one fits, the x and one row are cut to one line with an ellipsis. Its \"+N more\" is shown whole or the strip is not shown.\n\n**It never takes the pointer.** The pointer passes through a tooltip to the plot, so the readout follows every move and a click lands on the plot. To reach the tooltip, rest the pointer on the datum for a quarter of a second: the datum is then held while the pointer travels towards its tooltip and while it is over it. Leaving that path picks again at once; a pointer that moves without resting is never held. A click always acts on what is under the pointer, whatever is held.\n\n**A click or tap pins.** It selects the datum under the pointer (the `select` event) and pins its tooltip, or its strip. Pinned, the content takes the pointer and is exposed to assistive technology: each row is a button of at least 24 × 24 px that selects that series' value, and \"+N more\" becomes a button that shows the next rows. The rows are outside the tab order; from the keyboard, the plot's Up and Down reach the same series. The pin ends with Escape, a click elsewhere, a drag or pinch, or when its datum leaves the view. With `tooltip: false`, or in a plot with no readout, nothing is pinned; a click still selects. While Select range is pressed, a click sets an end of the zoom range instead, and neither pins nor selects ([zoom](updating.md#zoom)).\n\n## What you must supply\n\nAxylo can describe the data; only you know what it means.\n\n- **A meaningful name.** Give every chart a `title`, or, when the card around it already has a visible heading, `labelledBy` with that heading's id. A generated name (\"Line chart of sessions by day\") is a fallback and raises [AX140](diagnostics.md#ax140). A title can be hidden visually with `title: { text, visible: false }`, and its heading level set with `level` (2 to 6, default 3) to fit your page's outline.\n- **Series names.** For wide rows, give each field a `label`; for long rows, the series field's values are the names. They are part of every point's value text.\n- **Units.** Set `unit` (and `label`) on value fields, so that \"412\" is \"412 ms\".\n- **Context.** `description` adds what the data cannot say (\"The spike on 12 March was the payment provider outage.\") to the generated summary. It never removes the time zone or the statement of values left out.\n- **The page's structure.** Headings, landmarks and the order of content around the chart are yours.\n- **A pause for streaming compact tiles.** A full chart fed by `append` has its own Pause control. A compact chart has no toolbar, so it has none: if your tiles stream, give the reader a control of your own that stops appending, and append what arrived meanwhile on resume (WCAG 2.2.2).\n- **Text in other languages.** Every message the chart shows the reader can be replaced through `messages`; see the [translated example](options.md#messages). Diagnostics for developers keep their English wording. When the page's language is not English, the built-in English text is marked `lang=\"en\"`.\n\n[example: a11y-card](../examples/a11y-card.html)\n\n## What has and has not been verified\n\nAutomated checks establish structure: names, roles, focus order, contrast and text. They do not establish that a person using a screen reader understands a chart.\n\n- **Automated checks** are the library's acceptance tests, run in Chrome on Linux and in Playwright's builds of Firefox and WebKit. WebKit results are Playwright's WebKit, not Safari. What the acceptance checker recorded for the revision of this site is on the [conformance page](conformance.md).\n- **No screen-reader rehearsal was done for 1.0.** No screen reader has been run against these charts, so nothing in this documentation says what a screen reader speaks. The plot's pattern ([decision 0001](../../../docs/decisions/0001-plot-interaction-pattern.md)) and the rules for the tooltip and the focused point's text ([decision 0004](../../../docs/decisions/0004-tooltip-and-focused-text.md)) are untried with a screen reader and with a screen magnifier.\n- **No usability testing with disabled people.** Axylo is built by one person with no access to study participants, and no claim of usability testing with disabled users is made.\n- **Not run at all:** Safari itself (macOS and iOS), Edge, Windows, macOS, and phones.\n- **Limits of 1.0:** right-to-left text renders, but the layout is not mirrored. A chart inside a rotated, skewed or mirrored element draws, but pointing and tooltip placement are wrong there; inside a translated or scaled element (any positive scale) they are exact. Built-in text ships in English only.\n\n## Conformance\n\nThe [conformance table](conformance.md) lists the WCAG 2.2 success criteria the specification cites, what Axylo does for each, and what the acceptance checker recorded for them when this site was built.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
