/regional-sales.expected.json\\n?? rehearsal/fixtures/regional-sales.json\\n?? rehearsal/fixtures/signups.expected.json\\n?? rehearsal/fixtures/signups.json\\n?? rehearsal/grade.mjs\\n?? rehearsal/prompts/daily-revenue.md\\n?? rehearsal/prompts/daily-visits.md\\n?? rehearsal/prompts/regional-sales.md\\n?? rehearsal/prompts/signups.md\\n?? rehearsal/run.mjs\\n?? rehearsal/summary.mjs\\n?? rehearsal/table.mjs\\n?? rehearsal/test/fixtures.test.mjs\\n?? rehearsal/test/grade.browser.test.mjs\\n?? rehearsal/test/runner.test.mjs\\n?? rehearsal/vitest.config.mjs\\n?? rehearsal/workspace.mjs\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\nreviews/2026-11-19/wp/rehearsal-harness.events.jsonl:92:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_45\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc 'git diff --check -- rehearsal .gitignore && git status --short --untracked-files=all -- rehearsal .gitignore && node rehearsal/summary.mjs'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"?? rehearsal/README.md\\n?? rehearsal/agent.mjs\\n?? rehearsal/audit.mjs\\n?? rehearsal/files.mjs\\n?? rehearsal/fixtures/daily-revenue.expected.json\\n?? rehearsal/fixtures/daily-revenue.json\\n?? rehearsal/fixtures/daily-visits.expected.json\\n?? rehearsal/fixtures/daily-visits.json\\n?? rehearsal/fixtures/regional-sales.expected.json\\n?? rehearsal/fixtures/regional-sales.json\\n?? rehearsal/fixtures/signups.expected.json\\n?? rehearsal/fixtures/signups.json\\n?? rehearsal/grade.mjs\\n?? rehearsal/prompts/daily-revenue.md\\n?? rehearsal/prompts/daily-visits.md\\n?? rehearsal/prompts/regional-sales.md\\n?? rehearsal/prompts/signups.md\\n?? rehearsal/run.mjs\\n?? rehearsal/summary.mjs\\n?? rehearsal/table.mjs\\n?? rehearsal/test/fixtures.test.mjs\\n?? rehearsal/test/grade.browser.test.mjs\\n?? rehearsal/test/runner.test.mjs\\n?? rehearsal/vitest.config.mjs\\n?? rehearsal/workspace.mjs\\nDX12 agent rehearsal\\nRecord  Agent  Model  Task  Pass  Failed criteria\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\nreviews/2026-11-19/wp/rehearsal-harness.events.jsonl:101:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_50\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"Implemented DX12 in [rehearsal/](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/). It includes four fixed prompts and fixtures with hand-written expected rows, a [runner](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/run.mjs) for Codex and Claude, a [grader](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/grade.mjs) for Los Angeles and Tokyo, and a [summary](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/summary.mjs) of saved records.\\n\\n```sh\\nnode rehearsal/run.mjs --agent codex --model YOUR_MODEL_ID --effort high \\\\\\n  --task daily-visits --workdir /tmp/axylo-runs\\nnode rehearsal/summary.mjs\\n```\\n\\nA local stub verified invocation, transcript capture, model recording, and the summary. The non-browser tests passed.\\n\\n```text\\nTest Files  2 passed (2)\\n     Tests  16 passed (16)\\n```\\n\\nChrome failed to launch in the sandbox with `setsockopt: Operation not permitted`, so the 15 browser cases remain unverified. No live agent runs were made.\\n\\nThe visits fixture covers 30 calendar days with 27 observations. The grader accepts each missing day as absent or “No data.” Axylo automatically parses fields made entirely of decimal strings, so the unparsed-string failure test uses mixed numbers and strings in the combined fixture. The workspace copy preserves published examples and omits internal source snapshots. The audit checks paths visible in transcripts. The harness uses the existing public contracts.\"}}\nreviews/2026-11-19/wp/rehearsal-design.review.events.jsonl:8:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_2\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc 'cat reviews/2026-11-19/wp/rehearsal-harness.prompt.md reviews/2026-11-19/wp/rehearsal-harness.report.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"# Build the agent rehearsal harness (spec DX12)\\n\\nYou are working in /home/canarit/projects/axylo (Axylo, a TypeScript browser charting library). Read `AGENTS.md`, then the DX12 row of `docs/02-product-spec.md`, then the documentation pages an author starts from: `apps/docs/pages/getting-started.md`, `data.md`, `diagnostics.md`, `line.md`, `bar.md`. You own a new top-level directory `rehearsal/`; create nothing outside it, except one line in the root `.gitignore` if you need to ignore run workspaces. Do not edit `packages/**`, `apps/**`, `acceptance/**`, `tests/**`. You cannot commit (`.git` is read-only for you); the coordinator commits. The network is off: do not install anything (dev dependencies present: TypeScript, esbuild, playwright-core with system Chrome via `channel: 'chrome'`, Vitest). Do not run `npm run build` (it rewrites the shared `packages/axylo/dist`).\\n\\n## What DX12 asks\\n\\n\\\"A coding agent gets the first chart right. Given fixed prompts and trap fixtures (UTC-midnight ISO dates, numeric strings, unsorted rows, missing days, a misspelled field), an agent with only the published docs produces a chart with no error diagnostics that passes the reader criteria. Recorded as agent rehearsal, not as user evidence.\\\"\\n\\nThe harness runs a coding agent on a task, then grades what it wrote, automatically and the same way every time. The coordinator runs it; you build and test it. The agents under test are command-line coding agents (Codex CLI and Claude Code); you do not need to run them (and should not: they need the network), but the harness must invoke them.\\n\\n## Design (implement this; if a part cannot work as written, say why and do the closest honest thing)\\n\\n1. **Fixtures**, `rehearsal/fixtures/`: small realistic JSON files, one trap or more each, values chosen so that a mistake is visible in the result:\\n   - `daily-visits.json`: 30 days of `{ \\\"day\\\": \\\"2026-03-01T00:00:00Z\\\", \\\"visits\\\": 1234 }` (instants at UTC midnight that mean calendar days; read in America/Los_Angeles they fall on the previous day: the trap), with three days missing (absent rows, not nulls) and the rows shuffled.\\n   - `regional-sales.json`: quarterly sales by region where values are numeric strings (`\\\"1250.5\\\"`), one region per row field.\\n   - `signups.json`: daily signups where the field is spelled `signpus` in the data while the prompt says \\\"signups\\\" (the misspelled field: the agent must notice the diagnostic, AX101, and use the real key, not invent data).\\n   - one combined fixture with several traps at once.\\n   Write each fixture's expected reading in `rehearsal/fixtures/<name>.expected.json`: what the chart's data table (spec A4, the \\\"View as table\\\" table) must show, row by row, as calendar dates or categories and numbers, derived by hand from the fixture's meaning (missing days are absent or \\\"No data\\\", never 0; dates are the calendar days the data means; numbers are numbers). Do not derive expected values by running Axylo.\\n2. **Prompts**, `rehearsal/prompts/<id>.md`, one per task (4 to 6 tasks), written as a developer would ask, in plain words, naming the data file and the chart wanted (\\\"Show daily visits from data.json as a line chart titled 'Daily visits'.\\\"), never mentioning the trap. Each prompt also states the delivery contract (below) and that the only documentation available is in `docs/`.\\n3. **Workspace per run**, created by the runner outside the repository (in a directory the caller gives, default the session's scratch directory passed with `--workdir`): `docs/` = a copy of the built documentation site (`apps/docs/dist`, already built; copy, do not build), `node_modules/axylo` = the packed tarball unpacked (`tests/dist/.packed/axylo-0.0.0.tgz`, already packed), `data.json` = the fixture, `PROMPT.md` = the prompt, and a minimal `package.json` with `\\\"type\\\": \\\"module\\\"`. Nothing from the repository's source. The delivery contract: the agent writes `chart.js`, an ES module whose default export is `function render(element, data)` that draws the chart into `element` and returns the chart handle; it may use `import { line, bar, … } from 'axylo'`. It may run `node` (for example `validate` from `axylo`, which the docs describe) to check its work.\\n4. **Runner**, `rehearsal/run.mjs`: `node rehearsal/run.mjs --agent codex|claude --model <id> [--effort <e>] --task <id> --workdir <dir>`; prepares the workspace, runs the agent non-interactively in it with the prompt, with a time limit (default 20 minutes), and keeps the agent's full transcript:\\n   - codex: `codex exec -m <model> [-c model_reasoning_effort=\\\"<e>\\\"] -s workspace-write --skip-git-repo-check -C <workspace> --json - < PROMPT.md > transcript.jsonl` (the observed model and effort are in `~/.codex/sessions/**/rollout-*-<thread_id>.jsonl`, `turn_context`; record them; the thread id is the first `--json` event);\\n   - claude: `claude -p --model <model> --output-format stream-json --verbose --allowedTools \\\"Read,Write,Edit,Glob,Grep,Bash(node:*),Bash(ls:*),Bash(cat:*)\\\" < PROMPT.md > transcript.jsonl` with the workspace as the working directory (observed model: the `model` field of the assistant messages).\\n   - **Audit**: after the run, scan the transcript for any file path outside the workspace that the agent read or ran (command lines, tool inputs): a run that touched the Axylo repository or anything outside the workspace other than system tools is recorded as void, with the paths.\\n5. **Grader**, `rehearsal/grade.mjs` (the runner calls it; it can also be run alone on a workspace): bundles `chart.js` with esbuild for the browser (as a consumer would, `conditions: ['browser']`, the production condition), loads it in system Chrome (playwright-core, headless, `channel: 'chrome'`) into a page with `<div id=\\\"chart\\\" style=\\\"width:720px;height:400px\\\">`, calls `render`, and judges, in two time zones (`America/Los_Angeles` and `Asia/Tokyo`, via the context's `timezoneId`):\\n   - **no error diagnostics**: no console message carrying an Axylo error-level code (the registry `packages/axylo/src/diagnostics/registry.ts` gives levels; production prints errors and warnings once per code) and the chart is not in its error state; warnings and hints are listed in the record, not failed, except AX140 (no title) which fails the reader criterion below;\\n   - **reader criteria**: the figure has an accessible name that is the title the prompt asked for (not the generated fallback, AX140); opening \\\"View as table\\\" shows exactly the expected rows of `<fixture>.expected.json` in both time zones (dates as the calendar days, missing days not shown as 0, values as numbers formatted as the table formats them: compare numbers by value, not by string); no \\\"Values not shown\\\" note unless the expected file says one is right; the chart type is the one asked for.\\n   - If `chart.js` is missing, does not export `render`, or throws: fail, with the reason.\\n   - Output: `grade.json` in the workspace: `{ pass, criteria: [{ name, pass, detail }], diagnostics: [...], timezones: [...] }`.\\n6. **Record**: the runner copies the transcript, the agent's `chart.js`, `grade.json` and a `run.json` (agent, requested and observed model and effort, task, start and end times, duration, exit, void or not with paths) into `rehearsal/records/<date>-<agent>-<task>-<n>/` inside the repository, so the record survives a restart. `node rehearsal/summary.mjs` prints a table of all records (agent, model, task, pass, failed criteria).\\n\\n## Tests\\n\\nVitest tests in `rehearsal/test/` (run with `npx vitest run --root rehearsal` or a config there; do not change the root config): the grader on hand-written `chart.js` answers: a correct one per fixture passes; and mutations each fail the right criterion: UTC-midnight read as instants (Los Angeles shows the previous day), numeric strings not parsed, missing days filled with 0, the misspelled field used as given (AX101), no title, wrong chart type. The fixtures' expected files are checked against the fixtures by an independent small computation in the test (not by Axylo). The runner's audit on two synthetic transcripts (one clean, one that reads `/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/index.ts`). If the browser cannot be launched from your sandbox, write the browser tests anyway, mark which you could not run, and say so; the coordinator runs them.\\n\\n## Report (your final message)\\n\\nFiles; how to run one rehearsal; what you ran and saw (last lines); what you could not run; any place where the design above was not workable and what you did instead.\\n<!-- Author: Codex, requested gpt-6.2-sol xhigh; observed gpt-6.2-sol, effort xhigh (session rollout turn_context). workspace-write. Verified by the coordinator: npx vitest run --root rehearsal, 31 of 31 pass including the 15 browser grading cases the sandbox could not run. Design by the coordinator (Claude Opus 5.5); design review by gpt-6.2-astra follows. Saved by the coordinator. -->\\n\\nImplemented DX12 in [rehearsal/](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/). It includes four fixed prompts and fixtures with hand-written expected rows, a [runner](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/run.mjs) for Codex and Claude, a [grader](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/grade.mjs) for Los Angeles and Tokyo, and a [summary](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/summary.mjs) of saved records.\\n\\n```sh\\nnode rehearsal/run.mjs --agent codex --model YOUR_MODEL_ID --effort high \\\\\\n  --task daily-visits --workdir /tmp/axylo-runs\\nnode rehearsal/summary.mjs\\n```\\n\\nA local stub verified invocation, transcript capture, model recording, and the summary. The non-browser tests passed.\\n\\n```text\\nTest Files  2 passed (2)\\n     Tests  16 passed (16)\\n```\\n\\nChrome failed to launch in the sandbox with `setsockopt: Operation not permitted`, so the 15 browser cases remain unverified. No live agent runs were made.\\n\\nThe visits fixture covers 30 calendar days with 27 observations. The grader accepts each missing day as absent or “No data.” Axylo automatically parses fields made entirely of decimal strings, so the unparsed-string failure test uses mixed numbers and strings in the combined fixture. The workspace copy preserves published examples and omits internal source snapshots. The audit checks paths visible in transcripts. The harness uses the existing public contracts.\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\nreviews/2026-11-19/wp/rehearsal-design.review.events.jsonl:9:{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_3\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"rg -n 'DX12|\\\\\\\\| A[14] |\\\\\\\\| F(2|4|11|12) |reader criteria|agent rehearsal' docs/02-product-spec.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\nreviews/2026-11-19/wp/rehearsal-design.review.events.jsonl:10:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_3\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"rg -n 'DX12|\\\\\\\\| A[14] |\\\\\\\\| F(2|4|11|12) |reader criteria|agent rehearsal' docs/02-product-spec.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"96:| F2 | Missing values are never drawn as zero and never bridged unless the author asks. An explicit `null` is a gap, silently. An isolated value between two gaps is a visible point. A missing bar shows a \\\"no data\\\" mark at the baseline, distinct from a zero bar; a slot a series merely has no row for gets no bar and no mark. On a time or date axis with a regular step, an absent step is a gap and raises AX105; `missing: 'zero'` on the field turns absent and null values into zeros for counts. Tooltip, table and announcements say \\\"No data\\\". | `trend` with nulls and `trend` with the same 7 rows deleted render the same gap; `dirty` fixtures; property test that no segment connects across a missing index | R5 |\\n98:| F4 | Three temporal types, never mixed silently. **Calendar dates** (`2026-02-24`, `2026-02`, `2026`) read the same in every viewer time zone. **Instants** (epoch milliseconds declared `type: 'time'`, `Date` objects, ISO strings with an offset) are shown in `timeZone` (default: the viewer's), with the zone named in the tooltip heading and the description. **Wall-clock times** (date-time strings without an offset) are shown as written, with hint AX112. Instants that all fall on the same time of day, whole days apart, raise hint AX111 (\\\"these look like calendar dates\\\"). A bare number is never assumed to be an instant. | Four fixture variants in UTC, America/Los_Angeles and Asia/Kolkata: (a) date strings: identical text in all three; (b) ISO strings with `Z`, (c) epoch ms with `type: 'time'`, (d) `Date` objects: each zone's correct local text, zone visible, AX111 raised; with `timeZone: 'UTC'` or `type: 'date'`: identical. Plus: both DST transitions, a leap day, an unparseable date | R3 |\\n105:| F11 | Line, area and scatter are drawn in x order whatever the input order. On line, area and bar, two rows with the same x in one series are AX108 and that series is not drawn. On scatter, points sharing an x (or both coordinates) are ordinary data. | Shuffled `trend` renders identically; duplicate fixture | R5 |\\n106:| F12 | When a warning means values were left out or changed, the chart says so to the reader: a short note (\\\"Values not shown: 2. See table.\\\", worded so it reads correctly for any count) inside the figure and in the accessibility tree, in every build. | Production build given `dirty` shows the note | R5, R6 |\\n138:| A1 | Every chart has an accessible name: its `title`, or the heading named by `labelledBy`, or else a generated name (\\\"Line chart of Sessions by Day\\\") with AX140 raised in every build. The title's heading level is the author's (`level`), and it can be hidden visually. Every chart has a generated summary: type, series, x range with time zone, y range, each series' minimum, maximum and last value, and any omissions. `description` adds to the summary and never removes the zone or omission statements. Element ids are unique per chart. | Accessibility-tree snapshot for every fixture; a 24-chart page has no duplicate ids; axe `heading-order` | R11, R13, WCAG 1.1.1, 1.3.1, 2.4.6 |\\n141:| A4 | \\\"View as table\\\" shows the data as an HTML table built from the normalized data when opened: the title as caption, field labels with units as column headers, x values as row headers, values formatted as in F6. It covers the current x range, 100 rows per page, with every row reachable by paging or by jumping to an x value. It sits in a focusable scroll region no taller than the plot, and opening it does not move other charts. | Table content compared with fixture data, including a value near row 900,000 of 1,000,000; a 24-chart page layout test | R13, WCAG 1.3.1, 1.3.2, 3.3.1 |\\n234:| DX12 | A coding agent gets the first chart right. Given fixed prompts and trap fixtures (UTC-midnight ISO dates, numeric strings, unsorted rows, missing days, a misspelled field), an agent with only the published docs produces a chart with no error diagnostics that passes the reader criteria. Recorded as agent rehearsal, not as user evidence. | Scripted rehearsal at each release | D1, 1.2 |\\n286:| Test with screen-reader users, outside developers or design partners before release | One-person project with no participants. Recorded as a stated limit (3.8). Agent rehearsal (DX12) is used and labelled as such; the developer's screen-reader rehearsal (A3) was dropped from 1.0 by the owner and is stated as not done. |\\n289:| Remove the first-chart line-count criterion | Kept as a floor, and joined by DX12, which tests getting it right. |\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\nreviews/2026-11-19/wp/rehearsal-design.review.events.jsonl:15:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_5\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc 'cat -n rehearsal/README.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"     1\\t# DX12 agent rehearsal\\n     2\\t\\n     3\\tRun from the repository root with an authenticated Codex CLI or Claude Code installation, the existing development dependencies, system Chrome, and the built documentation and packed Axylo tarball.\\n     4\\t\\n     5\\t```sh\\n     6\\tnode rehearsal/run.mjs --agent codex --model YOUR_MODEL_ID --effort high --task daily-visits --workdir /tmp/axylo-runs\\n     7\\tnode rehearsal/summary.mjs\\n     8\\t```\\n     9\\t\\n    10\\tUse `--agent claude` for Claude Code. The runner applies `--effort` to Codex. Claude's stream reports the model and leaves the observed effort empty. The task IDs are `daily-visits`, `regional-sales`, `signups`, and `daily-revenue`. The runner uses the system temporary directory if `--workdir` is omitted and creates a fresh subdirectory for each run. The agent has 20 minutes by default. To override that limit, pass `--timeout-ms` in milliseconds.\\n    11\\t\\n    12\\tThe workspace draws from `apps/docs/dist` and `tests/dist/.packed/axylo-0.0.0.tgz`. The docs include their published examples and omit the internal repository snapshots linked from conformance evidence. The fixture and fixed prompt are the other inputs. The grader supplies the original fixture to `render`, so changes to an agent's scratch copy of `data.json` cannot change the data being graded.\\n    13\\t\\n    14\\tThe runner keeps each run under `rehearsal/records/` with the transcript, stderr, delivered chart if present, grade, and run metadata. The transcript audit checks recorded command lines and tool inputs for paths outside the workspace, with exceptions for system executables and `/dev/null`. It reports detected paths and any malformed transcript lines. It can only check accesses visible in the transcript and paths it can resolve. A void run remains in the summary with its grade for inspection. These records are agent rehearsal only.\\n    15\\t\\n    16\\tTo regrade a prepared workspace, use the workspace path printed by the runner.\\n    17\\t\\n    18\\t```sh\\n    19\\tnode rehearsal/grade.mjs --task daily-visits --workdir /path/to/run/workspace\\n    20\\t```\\n    21\\t\\n    22\\tThe grader uses two headless Chrome contexts with English (US) formatting and different time zones. The expected JSON gives table rows in reading order. An expected `null` is a missing observation. The table may omit a wholly missing row or label it “No data.” Chart types are read from Axylo's accessible plot description or generated summary. The grader opens the standard “View as table” control.\\n    23\\t\\n    24\\tThe runner and standalone grader return 0 for a pass, 1 for a failed grade or void run, and 2 for a setup or tool error.\\n    25\\t\\n    26\\t```sh\\n    27\\tnpx --no-install vitest run --root rehearsal\\n    28\\tnpx --no-install vitest run --root rehearsal --exclude '**/*.browser.test.mjs'\\n    29\\t```\\n    30\\t\\n    31\\tThe first command includes the Chrome tests. The second checks the fixtures, audit, agent invocation, workspace setup, and pre-browser grading without opening Chrome.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\nreviews/2026-11-19/wp/rehearsal-design.review.events.jsonl:16:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_6\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc 'cat rehearsal/files.mjs rehearsal/summary.mjs rehearsal/vitest.config.mjs'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"import { readFile, rename, rm, writeFile } from 'node:fs/promises';\\nimport { randomUUID } from 'node:crypto';\\nimport { isAbsolute, join, relative, sep } from 'node:path';\\nimport { fileURLToPath, pathToFileURL } from 'node:url';\\n\\nexport const rehearsal = fileURLToPath(new URL('.', import.meta.url));\\nexport const repository = fileURLToPath(new URL('..', import.meta.url));\\n\\nexport function inside(root, file) {\\n  const path = relative(root, file);\\n  return path !== '..' && !path.startsWith('..' + sep) && !isAbsolute(path);\\n}\\n\\nexport function taskFiles(task) {\\n  if (!task || !/^[a-z0-9]+(?:-[a-z0-9]+)*$/.test(task)) throw new Error('Supply a task ID from rehearsal/prompts.');\\n  return {\\n    prompt: join(rehearsal, 'prompts', task + '.md'),\\n    fixture: join(rehearsal, 'fixtures', task + '.json'),\\n    expected: join(rehearsal, 'fixtures', task + '.expected.json'),\\n  };\\n}\\n\\nexport async function readJson(file) {\\n  return JSON.parse(await readFile(file, 'utf8'));\\n}\\n\\nexport async function writeJson(file, value) {\\n  const temporary = file + '.' + randomUUID() + '.tmp';\\n  try {\\n    await writeFile(temporary, JSON.stringify(value, null, 2) + '\\\\n', { flag: 'wx' });\\n    await rename(temporary, file);\\n  } finally {\\n    await rm(temporary, { force: true });\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nexport function isMain(url) {\\n  return !!process.argv[1] && pathToFileURL(process.argv[1]).href === url;\\n}\\nimport { readdir } from 'node:fs/promises';\\nimport { join } from 'node:path';\\nimport { readJson, rehearsal } from './files.mjs';\\n\\nconst root = join(rehearsal, 'records');\\nconst entries = await readdir(root, { withFileTypes: true }).catch(error => {\\n  if (error.code === 'ENOENT') return [];\\n  throw error;\\n});\\nconst rows = [];\\nfor (const entry of entries.filter(entry => entry.isDirectory()).sort((a, b) => a.name.localeCompare(b.name))) {\\n  const directory = join(root, entry.name);\\n  try {\\n    const run = await readJson(join(directory, 'run.json'));\\n    const grade = await readJson(join(directory, 'grade.json'));\\n    const agentFailed = run.exit.code !== 0 || run.exit.timedOut || run.exit.error !== null;\\n    const failed = grade.criteria.filter(item => !item.pass).map(item => item.name);\\n    if (agentFailed) failed.unshift(run.exit.timedOut ? 'agent timeout' : 'agent exit');\\n    if (run.void) failed.unshift('outside workspace');\\n    rows.push([\\n      entry.name, run.agent, run.observed.model ?? `${run.requested.model} (requested)`, run.task,\\n      run.void ? 'void' : grade.pass && !agentFailed ? 'yes' : 'no', failed.join(', ') || '—',\\n    ]);\\n  } catch (error) {\\n    rows.push([entry.name, '—', '—', '—', 'incomplete', String(error)]);\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nconsole.log('DX12 agent rehearsal');\\nconst headings = ['Record', 'Agent', 'Model', 'Task', 'Pass', 'Failed criteria'];\\nconst widths = headings.map((heading, i) => Math.max(heading.length, ...rows.map(row => row[i].length)));\\nfor (const row of [headings, ...rows]) {\\n  console.log(row.map((value, i) => value.padEnd(widths[i])).join('  ').trimEnd());\\n}\\nimport { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url';\\nimport { defineConfig } from 'vitest/config';\\n\\nexport default defineConfig({\\n  root: fileURLToPath(new URL('.', import.meta.url)),\\n  cacheDir: '.vite',\\n  test: {\\n    include: ['test/**/*.test.mjs'],\\n    hookTimeout: 30000,\\n    testTimeout: 30000,\\n  },\\n});\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\nreviews/2026-11-19/wp/rehearsal-design.review.events.jsonl:26:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_11\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc 'cat -n rehearsal/run.mjs'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"     1\\timport { copyFile, lstat, mkdir, readFile } from 'node:fs/promises';\\n     2\\timport { tmpdir } from 'node:os';\\n     3\\timport { join } from 'node:path';\\n     4\\timport { parseArgs } from 'node:util';\\n     5\\timport { observeAgent, runAgent } from './agent.mjs';\\n     6\\timport { auditTranscript } from './audit.mjs';\\n     7\\timport { isMain, rehearsal, writeJson } from './files.mjs';\\n     8\\timport { grade } from './grade.mjs';\\n     9\\timport { prepareWorkspace } from './workspace.mjs';\\n    10\\t\\n    11\\tasync function reserveRecord(date, agent, task) {\\n    12\\t  const records = join(rehearsal, 'records');\\n    13\\t  await mkdir(records, { recursive: true });\\n    14\\t  for (let n = 1; ; n++) {\\n    15\\t    const record = join(records, `${date}-${agent}-${task}-${n}`);\\n    16\\t    try {\\n    17\\t      await mkdir(record);\\n    18\\t      return record;\\n    19\\t    } catch (error) {\\n    20\\t      if (error.code !== 'EEXIST') throw error;\\n    21\\t    }\\n    22\\t  }\\n    23\\t}\\n    24\\t\\n    25\\tasync function main() {\\n    26\\t  const { values } = parseArgs({\\n    27\\t    options: {\\n    28\\t      agent: { type: 'string' }, model: { type: 'string' }, effort: { type: 'string' }, task: { type: 'string' },\\n    29\\t      workdir: { type: 'string', default: tmpdir() }, 'timeout-ms': { type: 'string', default: '1200000' },\\n    30\\t    },\\n    31\\t  });\\n    32\\t  const { agent, model, effort, task, workdir } = values;\\n    33\\t  const timeoutMs = Number(values['timeout-ms']);\\n    34\\t  if (!['codex', 'claude'].includes(agent) || !model || !task ||\\n    35\\t      !Number.isSafeInteger(timeoutMs) || timeoutMs < 1 || timeoutMs > 2147483647) {\\n    36\\t    throw new Error('Usage: node rehearsal/run.mjs --agent codex|claude --model <id> [--effort <e>] --task <id> [--workdir <dir>] [--timeout-ms <ms>]');\\n    37\\t  }\\n    38\\t\\n    39\\t  const { workspace, runDirectory } = await prepareWorkspace(workdir, task);\\n    40\\t  console.log('Workspace: ' + workspace);\\n    41\\t  const start = new Date();\\n    42\\t  const { transcript, stderr, exit } = await runAgent(agent, { model, effort, workspace, runDirectory, timeoutMs });\\n    43\\t  const end = new Date();\\n    44\\t  const log = await readFile(transcript, 'utf8');\\n    45\\t  const [audit, observed] = await Promise.all([auditTranscript(log, workspace), observeAgent(agent, log)]);\\n    46\\t  const record = await reserveRecord(start.toISOString().slice(0, 10), agent, task);\\n    47\\t  await copyFile(transcript, join(record, 'transcript.jsonl'));\\n    48\\t  await copyFile(stderr, join(record, 'stderr.log'));\\n    49\\t\\n    50\\t  const answer = join(workspace, 'chart.js');\\n    51\\t  const answerStat = await lstat(answer).catch(error => {\\n    52\\t    if (error.code !== 'ENOENT') throw error;\\n    53\\t  });\\n    54\\t  if (answerStat?.isFile()) await copyFile(answer, join(record, 'chart.js'));\\n    55\\t  let result;\\n    56\\t  try {\\n    57\\t    result = await grade(workspace, task);\\n    58\\t  } catch (error) {\\n    59\\t    result = { pass: false, criteria: [{ name: 'grader', pass: false, detail: String(error) }], diagnostics: [], timezones: [] };\\n    60\\t    await writeJson(join(workspace, 'grade.json'), result);\\n    61\\t  }\\n    62\\t  await copyFile(join(workspace, 'grade.json'), join(record, 'grade.json'));\\n    63\\t  await writeJson(join(record, 'run.json'), {\\n    64\\t    kind: 'agent rehearsal', agent, task, requested: { model, effort: effort ?? null }, observed,\\n    65\\t    startedAt: start.toISOString(), endedAt: end.toISOString(), durationMs: end - start,\\n    66\\t    timeoutMs, exit, ...audit,\\n    67\\t  });\\n    68\\t  const agentFailed = exit.code !== 0 || exit.timedOut || exit.error !== null;\\n    69\\t  const status = audit.void ? 'void' : agentFailed || !result.pass ? 'fail' : 'pass';\\n    70\\t  console.log(`Agent rehearsal: ${status}. Record: ${record}`);\\n    71\\t  process.exitCode = exit.error || result.criteria.some(item => ['browser', 'grader'].includes(item.name)) ? 2\\n    72\\t    : status === 'pass' ? 0 : 1;\\n    73\\t}\\n    74\\t\\n    75\\tif (isMain(import.meta.url)) {\\n    76\\t  try {\\n    77\\t    await main();\\n    78\\t  } catch (error) {\\n    79\\t    console.error(String(error));\\n    80\\t    process.exitCode = 2;\\n    81\\t  }\\n    82\\t}\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\nreviews/2026-11-19/wp/rehearsal-design.review.events.jsonl:32:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_14\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"sed -n '1,30p' rehearsal/grade.mjs && sed -n '50,130p' rehearsal/run.mjs\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"import { lstat, readFile, realpath } from 'node:fs/promises';\\nimport { basename, join, resolve } from 'node:path';\\nimport { parseArgs } from 'node:util';\\nimport { build } from 'esbuild';\\nimport { chromium } from 'playwright-core';\\nimport { inside, isMain, readJson, repository, taskFiles, writeJson } from './files.mjs';\\nimport { compareRows } from './table.mjs';\\n\\nconst zones = ['America/Los_Angeles', 'Asia/Tokyo'];\\nconst timeout = 5000;\\nconst origin = 'https://axylo-rehearsal.invalid';\\nconst criteriaNames = ['render', 'no error diagnostics', 'accessible name', 'table rows', 'reader note', 'chart type'];\\nconst criterion = (name, pass, detail) => ({ name, pass, detail });\\n\\nasync function registryLevels() {\\n  const registry = await readFile(join(repository, 'packages/axylo/src/diagnostics/registry.ts'), 'utf8');\\n  const levels = new Map([...registry.matchAll(/^\\\\s*(AX\\\\d+): \\\\{ level: '(error|warning|hint)'/gm)]\\n    .map(([, code, level]) => [code, level]));\\n  if (levels.get('AX101') !== 'error' || levels.get('AX140') !== 'warning') {\\n    throw new Error('Could not read diagnostic levels from the Axylo registry.');\\n  }\\n  return levels;\\n}\\n\\nasync function bundle(workspace, data) {\\n  const result = await build({\\n    absWorkingDir: workspace,\\n    stdin: {\\n      resolveDir: workspace,\\n      contents: `\\n  const answer = join(workspace, 'chart.js');\\n  const answerStat = await lstat(answer).catch(error => {\\n    if (error.code !== 'ENOENT') throw error;\\n  });\\n  if (answerStat?.isFile()) await copyFile(answer, join(record, 'chart.js'));\\n  let result;\\n  try {\\n    result = await grade(workspace, task);\\n  } catch (error) {\\n    result = { pass: false, criteria: [{ name: 'grader', pass: false, detail: String(error) }], diagnostics: [], timezones: [] };\\n    await writeJson(join(workspace, 'grade.json'), result);\\n  }\\n  await copyFile(join(workspace, 'grade.json'), join(record, 'grade.json'));\\n  await writeJson(join(record, 'run.json'), {\\n    kind: 'agent rehearsal', agent, task, requested: { model, effort: effort ?? null }, observed,\\n    startedAt: start.toISOString(), endedAt: end.toISOString(), durationMs: end - start,\\n    timeoutMs, exit, ...audit,\\n  });\\n  const agentFailed = exit.code !== 0 || exit.timedOut || exit.error !== null;\\n  const status = audit.void ? 'void' : agentFailed || !result.pass ? 'fail' : 'pass';\\n  console.log(`Agent rehearsal: ${status}. Record: ${record}`);\\n  process.exitCode = exit.error || result.criteria.some(item => ['browser', 'grader'].includes(item.name)) ? 2\\n    : status === 'pass' ? 0 : 1;\\n}\\n\\nif (isMain(import.meta.url)) {\\n  try {\\n    await main();\\n  } catch (error) {\\n    console.error(String(error));\\n    process.exitCode = 2;\\n  }\\n}\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\nreviews/2026-11-19/wp/rehearsal-design.review.events.jsonl:39:{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_18\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"rg -n -i 'reader criteria|readers?|rehearsal|DX12' docs/02-product-spec.md | head -110\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\nreviews/2026-11-19/wp/rehearsal-design.review.events.jsonl:40:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_18\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"rg -n -i 'reader criteria|readers?|rehearsal|DX12' docs/02-product-spec.md | head -110\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"5:Based on the findings in [01-landscape.md](01-landscape.md). Finding IDs (R1–R16 for readers, D1–D10 for developers) refer to that document. Criterion IDs defined here are F, L, I, A (reader), DX (developer) and P (performance).\\n9:**Axylo is the everyday chart you can ship without checking it.** The smallest correct call produces a chart that represents the data faithfully, stays readable at any width and in any color scheme, and works with a keyboard and a screen reader. When the data is wrong, Axylo says so, to the developer and to the reader, instead of drawing a guess.\\n21:| Chart.js | Simple API, permissive license, widest reach | Keyboard and screen-reader access without author work; reduced-motion support; a tooltip that appears wherever the pointer is; large data without expert options; a stated time zone on every instant |\\n23:| Highcharts | The best reader experience of the ten | The same class of accessibility with no module to import and no commercial license; reduced-motion support; errors that name the field and the row |\\n31:A developer adding charts to a product or an internal dashboard: data arrives as rows from an API, the chart is one component among many on the page, and the person writing it is not a visualization specialist and will not test it with a screen reader. Coding agents writing that code are the same audience: they benefit from typed fields, one logical data model and diagnostics that say what to change.\\n40:| Say it, do not guess | Anything Axylo cannot draw faithfully becomes a diagnostic with a code, a location and a fix. If values were left out, the reader is told too. |\\n42:| Everything a reader needs is on; nothing else is | Title, units, legend, tooltip, keyboard, description and table are on. Entry animation, a marker on every point of a dense line, and decorative toolbars are not. |\\n74:| W6 | Read it another way | Keyboard, screen reader, table view, high contrast, reduced motion, 400% zoom, color-vision deficiency. |\\n87:## 3. Acceptance criteria: the reader's experience\\n97:| F3 | Input that cannot be plotted as given is reported, never guessed. The diagnostic registry (section 3.5) fixes each case's level, what is drawn and what the reader sees. | One test per code; the combined `dirty` fixture and the four one-bad-value fixtures yield exactly the codes listed in section 3.5 | R5, D5 |\\n104:| F10 | Stacks place positive and negative values on their own sides of zero, and the axis covers both totals. The readout's total is the sum when every component has the same sign; when signs are mixed it gives the positive total and the negative total, the two ends the reader sees, not their net. A missing component is drawn with zero height and the stack is marked \\\"incomplete\\\" in tooltip, table and announcements. A component is missing whenever its series has no value at that x, whatever the reason: a null, an absent step, or a series that simply has no row there (a weekly series stacked with a daily one is incomplete on the days between its rows; the owner's decision, 2026-11-18: a stack must never show a total made of values the data does not contain). A stacked series' line follows its band, so inside a stack it is not joined across such a slot. | Property test on stacked geometry; mixed-sign and incomplete fixtures | R5 |\\n106:| F12 | When a warning means values were left out or changed, the chart says so to the reader: a short note (\\\"Values not shown: 2. See table.\\\", worded so it reads correctly for any count) inside the figure and in the accessibility tree, in every build. | Production build given `dirty` shows the note | R5, R6 |\\n112:| L1 | No visible text overlaps other text or is clipped, and no label disappears without the reader being told. Remedies, in order: thin (time, date, numeric and `ordered` category axes only); wrap to at most three lines; rotate up to 45°; truncate in the middle, keeping every label distinct, with the full text in tooltip and table. If labels still do not fit, a visible note (\\\"Some labels hidden. See table.\\\") appears and AX143 suggests horizontal bars. Axylo never changes a chart's orientation on its own. | In a browser, as painted text and not in a model (in the engines of section 2.3 where results are recorded), after fonts load: for widths 240–1,920 px and generated label sets, zero intersecting text boxes (1 px tolerance), zero text outside the figure, truncated labels pairwise distinct. Repeated with the WCAG 1.4.12 text-spacing overrides, 200% text-only zoom, and a font that loads late | R2, WCAG 1.4.12, 1.4.4 |\\n140:| A3 | Moving focus to a point conveys its text (series, x, value with unit, position) through the focused element's value text: the plot is a native range control whose `aria-valuetext` describes the focused point (decision 0001; earlier drafts said \\\"accessible name or a polite live region\\\", which the chosen pattern does not use, and A7 forbids announcements for navigation other than this). On touch screen readers, every point is reachable by swiping, or the table is reachable within two gestures. | Accessibility-tree snapshot after each key. No screen-reader rehearsal is part of 1.0 (the owner's decision, 2026-11-18): what a reader actually speaks on NVDA, VoiceOver or TalkBack has not been checked, and the conformance table says so. The script for such a rehearsal is kept in decision 0001 for when one is done. | R12, R13, WCAG 4.1.2 |\\n144:| A7 | Data arriving does not speak. Announcements happen only for the reader's own navigation, and for a change of state (L7). While data streams, keyboard focus stays on the same datum until it leaves the window. | Stream test counting live-region changes with and without focus; 24 concurrent streams | R12 |\\n151:Level decides delivery. **Error**: the chart or a series cannot be drawn; the chart shows its error state. **Warning**: something is not as the author gave it; where that means values were left out or changed (F12), the reader sees the note, and the registry's rows say which codes those are (a warning about an option, a name, a colour or a tick label shows none). **Hint**: advice for the author. Errors and warnings print once per code per page in every build; hints print in development builds only. `strict: true` throws on errors and warnings found while validating a create, `update` or `append` call, synchronously from that call. A diagnostic that can only arise later (a contrast check after the page's CSS changes, a custom formatter that throws when a table page is built, a label that stops fitting after a resize) is reported when it is found and is never thrown. Every diagnostic names the chart; those about data name the field and the first row indexes in the caller's own data.\\n174:| AX143 | Hint | Labels hidden after every remedy | Note shown to the reader |\\n182:| AX160 | Warning | A custom formatter threw | The default format is used for that value. A value formatter that throws changes how a value reads, so the reader's note (F12) is shown; a tick formatter that throws only changes an axis label, which is not a value left out or changed, so it is reported without a note (and so the note can never change which ticks fit) |\\n217:Automated checks establish structure: names, roles, focus order, contrast, text. They do not establish that a screen-reader user finds a chart understandable. 1.0 has no evidence for that: no screen reader was run against it, not even by the developer (the owner's decision, 2026-11-18; the rehearsal on five reader and browser pairs that earlier drafts of this section planned was not done). Axylo is built by one person with no access to study participants, so no claim of usability testing with disabled users is made, and the conformance table says both things in those words. Reviewers proposed participant testing as a release gate; it is recorded here as a known limit instead.\\n234:| DX12 | A coding agent gets the first chart right. Given fixed prompts and trap fixtures (UTC-midnight ISO dates, numeric strings, unsorted rows, missing days, a misspelled field), an agent with only the published docs produces a chart with no error diagnostics that passes the reader criteria. Recorded as agent rehearsal, not as user evidence. | Scripted rehearsal at each release | D1, 1.2 |\\n263:| Diagnostics are a first-class feature with codes | R5: none of the ten named the field, the row or the problem. | Kept. Severity and delivery rewritten (3.5): readers are told too, and production builds are not silent. |\\n268:| Label fitting is a tested invariant with a visible fallback | R2. | Changed. The absolute \\\"everything always fits\\\" was infeasible in a 240 px tile; it is now \\\"nothing overlaps, and nothing disappears without the reader being told\\\". Rotation comes before truncation. |\\n286:| Test with screen-reader users, outside developers or design partners before release | One-person project with no participants. Recorded as a stated limit (3.8). Agent rehearsal (DX12) is used and labelled as such; the developer's screen-reader rehearsal (A3) was dropped from 1.0 by the owner and is stated as not done. |\\n288:| Switch to horizontal bars automatically when labels do not fit | Changing orientation changes what the chart says at a glance; Axylo suggests it and tells the reader what is hidden. |\\n289:| Remove the first-chart line-count criterion | Kept as a floor, and joined by DX12, which tests getting it right. |\\n298:| Which keyboard and screen-reader pattern for the plot? | Decide from the M1 spike: three candidate patterns on the `trend` chart, rehearsed on the five pairs. | M1 record, confirmed in M4. |\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
