{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_41","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '30,55p' apps/docs/pages/getting-started.md && sed -n '18,42p' apps/docs/pages/bar.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_41","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '30,55p' apps/docs/pages/getting-started.md && sed -n '18,42p' apps/docs/pages/bar.md\"","aggregated_output":"\nPoint at a chart, or press Tab to reach its plot and use the arrow keys, or open its table. The only required options are `data` and the fields: `x` and `y` (and `series` for long rows), or `label` and `value` for a donut. `title` is not required, but without it (or `labelledBy`) the chart gets a generated name and warning [AX140](diagnostics.md#ax140).\n\n## Using Axylo in a framework\n\nNo framework adapter ships in 1.0; adapters for React, Vue, Svelte and others are planned after 1.0. Axylo is called from any framework's own hooks: create the chart when its element exists, call `update()` when your data or options change, and call `destroy()` when the element goes away. Creating, destroying and creating again on the same element is supported; it is the sequence a framework's development mode produces.\n\nThe lifecycle as three plain functions:\n\n[example: lifecycle](../examples/lifecycle.ts)\n\n## Daily data: five things to get right\n\nThe first chart above has months as calendar dates and numbers as numbers. Data from an API is often less tidy. Here is a daily metric as many back ends send it, and the chart that reads it correctly:\n\n[example: daily-sessions](../examples/daily-sessions.ts)\n\n1. **Days sent as UTC midnights.** For daily totals sent as `'2026-02-24T00:00:00Z'` (or as epoch milliseconds of a UTC midnight), use `x: { field: 'day', type: 'date' }`. Without it they are instants, shown in the viewer's time zone, so west of UTC each value appears on the day before, with only hint [AX111](diagnostics.md#ax111). Strings such as `'2026-02-24'` need no `type`. See [the three kinds of time](data.md#three-kinds-of-time).\n2. **Numbers sent as strings.** For decimal strings, use `y: { field: 'sessions', parse: 'number' }`. They are converted to JavaScript numbers, which are exact for integers up to 9,007,199,254,740,991; a larger count is rounded without a warning, so check exact counts with `Number.isSafeInteger` first. A field that mixes numbers and strings is not converted at all. See [numbers sent as strings](data.md#numbers-sent-as-strings).\n3. **Row order.** Pass rows in any order. Axylo sorts dated rows by x and keeps each value with its own row; categories keep the order in which they first appear.\n4. **A day with no data.** When you know a day is missing, send it, with `sessions: null`. Axylo can infer a missing day only between two rows of a series that has at least three rows at a regular step, and never before the first row or after the last; with two rows, or an irregular series, the line runs straight across the missing day. See [missing values](data.md#missing-values).\n5. **A misspelled field.** On error [AX101](diagnostics.md#ax101), compare the field you named with the keys the message lists, which come from your rows, and use the suggested spelling when one is offered. With no rows at all the keys cannot be checked, so the error appears once data arrives. With typed rows in TypeScript the typo does not compile.\n\n## What you get without asking\n\n| Feature | What it does |\n```\n\n| Option | On a bar chart |\n|---|---|\n| `x` | The categories (or dates, numbers). Categories keep the order of the data. `ordered: true` says they have a natural order (months, age bands), which allows thinning their labels. |\n| `y`, `series` | One value field (with `series` for long rows), or a list of fields. Several series make groups. |\n| `layout` | `'grouped'` (default) puts the series side by side; `'stacked'` stacks them. |\n| `horizontal` | `true` draws the bars from the left, with the categories down the side. Default: `false`. A `y` reference then runs up and down the plot, and an `x` reference across it. |\n| `zoom` | On date, time and number x only. A category axis does not zoom. |\n| `reference`, `compact` | As on a line chart. |\n| `domain` on `y` | Widened to include zero, with [AX110](diagnostics.md#ax110). |\n| Common options | `title`, `labelledBy`, `subtitle`, `description`, `state`, `legend`, `tooltip`, `table`, `colors`, `messages`, `locale`, `strict`, `onDiagnostic`. |\n\nNot on a bar chart: `stacked` (that is `layout: 'stacked'` here), `label` and `value` (donut only).\n\n## How a bar chart behaves\n\n| Behaviour | Bar |\n|---|---|\n| Pointer | The band under the pointer: the whole category, not only the bar. The tooltip lists every series in that category; a click pins it. See [reading values with a pointer](accessibility.md#reading-values-with-a-pointer). |\n| Keyboard | By category, then series. |\n| Zoom | Only on date, time and number x, as on a line. On a category axis `zoom()` changes nothing and is reported ([AX150](diagnostics.md#ax150)). |\n| Baseline | The value axis always includes zero; an author `domain` without zero is widened ([AX110](diagnostics.md#ax110)). Grouped bars grow from zero. |\n| Missing values | A missing bar (a `null` or absent value, or a step Axylo infers is missing) gets a small \"no data\" mark, distinct from a zero bar: at the baseline for grouped bars, at the edge of the stack for stacked ones. A slot a series simply has no row for, as for a weekly series beside daily ones, gets no bar and no mark. |\n| Stacking | Positive parts stack up from zero, negative parts down from it, and the axis covers both. With mixed signs each bar shows two totals, of its positive and of its negative parts, and the readout names both (\"Total 165 k USD and -60 k USD\" in the example below); the bar does not show their difference. A missing part has zero height and the total is marked \"incomplete\"; a part is missing whenever its series has no value at that x, also when the series simply has no row there. |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
